window.TOPICS={"se":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","hook":"Software fails at scale because complexity, cost and change go unmanaged — this unit defines the discipline that manages them.","steps":[{"t":"Software is more than code","d":"Programs plus documentation plus configuration data, so delivery is not finished when the code compiles."},{"t":"Quality is a trade, not a checklist","d":"Maintainability, dependability, efficiency and acceptability pull against each other; every project chooses a balance."},{"t":"Amateur practice collapses at scale","d":"Heterogeneity, legacy systems, security and business change defeat solo programming, so teams and process appear."},{"t":"That is engineering, not science","d":"Computer science asks what can be computed; software engineering asks how to build it reliably with money and people."},{"t":"The boundary is wider still","d":"System engineering covers hardware, software, people and process; software engineering is one part of it."},{"t":"Which is why ethics is examined","d":"Engineers decide about safety, privacy and disclosure, so a code of conduct is part of the discipline."}],"keys":[{"k":"Software","v":"Programs, documentation and configuration data — not just executable code."},{"k":"Four core quality attributes","v":"Maintainability, dependability, efficiency and acceptability; the rest are specialisations of these."},{"k":"Software engineering","v":"Engineering discipline covering all aspects of production, from specification to maintenance, using systematic quantified methods."},{"k":"SE vs computer science","v":"SE is practical, process-driven and economic; CS is theoretical, algorithmic and mathematical."},{"k":"SE vs system engineering","v":"System engineering covers hardware, software, people and process; SE is one component."},{"k":"Maintenance share","v":"About 60 per cent of lifetime cost, which is why maintainability dominates design."},{"k":"Heterogeneity","v":"Challenge of integrating diverse, independently built systems, often over distributed networks."},{"k":"Professional software","v":"Built by teams for sale or organisational use, documented, maintainable and designed for change."},{"k":"Internet software engineering","v":"Service-oriented, incrementally delivered, reused, cloud-hosted, multi-tenant and continuously deployed."},{"k":"ACM/IEEE ethics","v":"Public interest, client and employer, product quality, judgement, management, profession, colleagues, self."}],"traps":["Defining software as only programs, which loses documentation and configuration marks.","Listing quality attributes without saying they conflict and must be traded.","Treating computer science and software engineering as synonyms in a comparison question.","Reciting the eight ethics headings with no concrete example of a breach."],"asks":["Differentiate between software engineering and computer science.","What are the good characteristics of software? Explain.","Describe software engineering ethics that needs to be followed. Why is it important?","Differentiate between software engineering and system engineering.","Write short notes on internet software engineering."],"skel":["Definition: programs + documentation + configuration data","Attributes: maintainability, dependability, efficiency, acceptability","SE = systematic, quantified, economic, team-based production","SE builds software; CS studies computation; system engineering spans both","Challenges: heterogeneity, business change, security, scale, legacy","Ethics: public interest, confidentiality, competence, IP, honest disclosure"],"answer":"Software engineering is the discipline of producing software to a cost, schedule and quality target using systematic, disciplined and quantifiable methods. Software is not just code: it is programs plus documentation plus configuration data, and its quality is judged by maintainability, dependability, efficiency and acceptability. These attributes pull against each other, so every project trades them. It matters because complexity, change, heterogeneity and legacy systems make amateur programming fail at scale; professional development adds teams, process, documentation and configuration control. Computer science asks what can be computed and how efficiently; software engineering asks how to build it reliably with people and money. System engineering is wider still — hardware, software, people and process — and software engineering is one part of it. Ethics follows directly: confidentiality, competence, respect for intellectual property and honest disclosure of risk, because engineering decisions affect safety and privacy."},{"n":2,"name":"Software Processes","hook":"Every process model is one answer to a single question: how much can you know before you start building?","steps":[{"t":"Waterfall freezes everything first","d":"One downward pass: requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, maintenance, each phase signed off before the next."},{"t":"That assumption breaks on real projects","d":"Requirements move, paper reviews miss interface faults, nothing runs until late, so all risk lands in testing."},{"t":"Incremental buys back the feedback","d":"Build usable slices, prioritised by value, so change is absorbed early and the customer sees working software soon."},{"t":"But slices need glue","d":"Shared architecture, refactoring and repeated integration cost effort; large teams and fixed-price contracts resist the model."},{"t":"Reuse is the third escape route","d":"Integration and configuration assembles COTS components, trading exact requirement fit for speed and proven reliability."},{"t":"So the paper asks you to choose","d":"No model wins outright; requirement stability, size, contract type and safety criticality decide, which is the examinable judgement."}],"keys":[{"k":"Software process","v":"A set of related activities and results that produce a software product."},{"k":"Four process activities","v":"Specification, design and implementation, validation, and evolution."},{"k":"Waterfall","v":"Linear sequential model with distinct phases and formal sign-off between them; needs stable requirements."},{"k":"Incremental development","v":"System delivered as prioritised slices, each adding usable functionality; may be plan-driven or agile."},{"k":"Integration and configuration","v":"Reuse-oriented process assembling and configuring existing components and COTS products."},{"k":"Throw-away prototyping","v":"Prototype built to clarify unclear requirements, then discarded before real development."},{"k":"Evolutionary prototyping","v":"Prototype refined into the delivered system; suits uncertain requirements, risks poor structure."},{"k":"CMMI levels","v":"Initial, managed, defined, quantitatively managed, optimising — a process maturity ladder."},{"k":"Refactoring","v":"Restructuring code without changing behaviour, to keep incremental designs from decaying."},{"k":"Process improvement","v":"Measuring and changing the process itself, through maturity models, IDEAL, PDCA and retrospectives."}],"traps":["Calling waterfall simply old and bad; it suits stable requirements and fixed-price contracts.","Treating incremental, iterative and agile as the same model.","Listing pros and cons with no link to how stable the requirements are.","Forgetting that integration and configuration can force requirements to be renegotiated."],"asks":["Explain the different software life cycle models and compare them with advantages and disadvantages.","What is software process model? Discuss waterfall model with its merits and demerits.","Differentiate between incremental development and integration and configuration model.","Differentiate between evolutionary and throw-away prototyping model.","What do you understand by software process model? Explain different software process activities.","Why do you think software needs to be changed? Explain one model to cope with change."],"skel":["Process = activities + results producing software","Activities: specification, design and implementation, validation, evolution","Waterfall: linear, documented, stable requirements only","Incremental: prioritised slices, early value, needs architecture","Reuse: assemble COTS, faster but compromises requirements","Coping with change: prototyping, incremental delivery, refactoring, configuration management"],"answer":"The waterfall model arranges the process as a single downward pass: requirements analysis, system and software design, implementation and unit testing, integration and system testing, then operation and maintenance. Each phase produces a signed-off document that feeds the next, and the system is delivered once. Its strength is visibility: milestones, documentation and a fixed price are easy to contract around, so it suits stable requirements, well-understood problems and hardware-coupled projects. Its weakness is the assumption behind it. Requirements rarely stay still, paper reviews cannot expose interface and performance faults, and no working software exists until late, so all integration risk lands in testing, where change is most expensive and feedback arrives when nothing can be cheaply altered. Incremental development therefore builds usable slices, absorbing change and delivering value early, at the cost of shared architecture, refactoring and repeated integration effort."},{"n":3,"name":"Agile Software Development","hook":"Agile is what you get when change is cheaper to absorb than to predict.","steps":[{"t":"Plan-driven assumes a frozen specification","d":"Requirements are agreed once, design is completed up front, and change passes through formal control."},{"t":"For most products that assumption is false","d":"Market and technology move faster than the document, so a frozen specification records yesterday's guess."},{"t":"So invert the cost of change","d":"Keep the feedback loop to two or four weeks, deliver working software each time, and welcome late requirements."},{"t":"Short loops demand engineering discipline","d":"Test-driven development, refactoring, continuous integration and pair programming stop the design rotting under constant change."},{"t":"Discipline needs roles and artefacts","d":"Scrum supplies Product Owner, Scrum Master and team, a backlog and increment, plus planning, stand-up, review and retrospective."},{"t":"Hence plan-driven versus agile is the question","d":"Regulated, contracted and safety-critical work still needs plans; scaling agile fails without architecture and coordination."}],"keys":[{"k":"Agile Manifesto","v":"Values individuals, working software, collaboration and response to change over process, documentation, contracts and plans."},{"k":"Sprint","v":"Timeboxed iteration, usually two to four weeks, ending in a potentially shippable increment."},{"k":"Product backlog","v":"Prioritised list of everything wanted in the product, owned and ordered by the Product Owner."},{"k":"Scrum roles","v":"Product Owner sets value, Scrum Master removes impediments, development team self-organises delivery."},{"k":"Velocity","v":"Story points completed per sprint; a planning forecast, not a productivity target."},{"k":"XP practices","v":"Test-first development, pair programming, continuous integration, refactoring, small releases, simple design, on-site customer."},{"k":"User story","v":"Short requirement framed as role, goal and benefit, with acceptance criteria for testing."},{"k":"Burndown chart","v":"Visible plot of remaining work against time within a sprint or release."},{"k":"Scaling problems","v":"Distributed teams, fixed contracts, regulation, architecture and cross-team coordination resist agile at size."}],"traps":["Claiming agile means no documentation; it means documentation that earns its keep.","Equating agile with Scrum, when Scrum is one method among XP, Kanban and DSDM.","Treating velocity as a productivity measure to be maximised.","Ignoring that safety-critical and contracted work still needs plan-driven elements."],"asks":["Differentiate between plan driven and agile development. Explain agile project management.","What are the principles of agile development? Explain agile development techniques.","Explain the Agile software development and its applications.","Compare between agile software development with prototyping software development.","Explain the agile development and its techniques.","Write short notes on agile project management."],"skel":["Agile values: working software, collaboration, change, individuals","Plan-driven fixes scope; agile fixes time and quality","Scrum: PO, SM, team; backlog, sprint, increment","Ceremonies: planning, stand-up, review, retrospective","XP: TDD, pairing, CI, refactoring, small releases","Scaling fails without architecture, contracts and coordination"],"answer":"Plan-driven development fixes requirements, produces heavy documentation and controls change formally, so it suits contracts, safety-critical systems and large distributed teams. Agile assumes requirements will change and reduces the cost of change instead: short iterations, working software as the measure of progress, customer collaboration and emergent design. In practice agile means Scrum. The Product Owner orders a product backlog; the team pulls the highest-value items into a two-to-four week sprint backlog during sprint planning; a Scrum Master removes impediments; a daily stand-up synchronises work; the sprint ends in a review of the increment and a retrospective on the process. Velocity and burndown make progress visible. Agile only works with engineering discipline — test-driven development, refactoring and continuous integration — otherwise constant change rots the design. Regulated or contract-bound work still needs plan-driven elements, which is why scaling fails without architecture."},{"n":4,"name":"Requirements Engineering","hook":"Most failed systems were built exactly to specification — of the wrong requirement.","steps":[{"t":"Requirements come in two layers","d":"User requirements state business needs in natural language; system requirements state detailed, testable functions and constraints for developers."},{"t":"Each layer carries three types","d":"Functional: what it does. Non-functional: how well. Domain: rules inherited from the field, such as VAT or aviation safety."},{"t":"Non-functional requirements decide architecture","d":"'Fast' is untestable; 'search under two seconds at 500 users' constrains design, so every NFR needs a metric."},{"t":"You cannot specify what you never elicited","d":"Interviews, observation, scenarios and prototypes surface the real need, because users report solutions rather than problems."},{"t":"So the draft must be validated","d":"Reviews, prototypes and test-case generation check validity, consistency, completeness, realism and verifiability before code exists."},{"t":"And change never stops","d":"Baselines, traceability and a change control board make each change visible, priced and reversible — the reason management is a phase."}],"keys":[{"k":"Requirement","v":"A statement of what the system must do, or a property it must have."},{"k":"User requirement","v":"High-level natural-language statement for customers and managers, without technical detail."},{"k":"System requirement","v":"Detailed, structured and testable description of functions and constraints for developers."},{"k":"Functional requirement","v":"Service, input, output, behaviour or error handling the system must provide."},{"k":"Non-functional requirement","v":"Quality or constraint such as performance, security, availability, usability, maintainability or legality."},{"k":"Domain requirement","v":"Constraint derived from the application domain, e.g. VAT calculation or aviation safety rules."},{"k":"Elicitation techniques","v":"Interviews, questionnaires, observation, document analysis, focus groups, scenarios, prototyping, workshops."},{"k":"Good SRS properties","v":"Clear, unambiguous, complete, consistent, verifiable, traceable, feasible, prioritised, uniquely identified."},{"k":"Validation checks","v":"Validity, consistency, completeness, realism and verifiability of the stated requirements."},{"k":"Traceability matrix","v":"Links each requirement to design, code and tests so impact of change can be assessed."}],"traps":["Calling 'the system shall be user-friendly' a good non-functional requirement; it has no metric.","Assuming users can state their needs rather than their preferred solution.","Mixing functional examples into the non-functional list in a comparison answer.","Describing elicitation as one interview instead of a set of complementary techniques."],"asks":["Differentiate between functional and non-functional requirements. Explain the requirement engineering process.","What is requirements elicitation? Explain different requirements elicitation techniques.","What are the desirable characteristics of a good SRS document? Explain with example.","What is the difference between functional and non-functional requirement? Which is more critical and why?","Explain the main activities used in requirements engineering.","Briefly explain functional, non-functional, and domain requirements."],"skel":["User requirements: business language; system requirements: testable detail","Functional = what; non-functional = how well; domain = field rules","Every NFR needs a measurable metric","Process: elicitation, analysis, specification, validation, management","Elicitation: interviews, observation, scenarios, prototypes, workshops","Change control: baseline, traceability, impact analysis, CCB"],"answer":"Functional requirements state what the system must do — services, inputs, outputs, behaviour and error handling. Non-functional requirements constrain how well it does it: performance, security, availability, usability, maintainability and legal constraints. Non-functional requirements are usually more critical because they decide the architecture: 'search results in under two seconds for 500 concurrent users' constrains design, whereas 'the system shall search books' does not. A vague NFR is untestable, so each needs a measurable metric. The requirements engineering process has four activities. Elicitation gathers needs through interviews, observation, scenarios, workshops and prototypes. Analysis and negotiation resolve conflicts and set priority. Specification documents user requirements at a high level and system requirements in detail, using structured natural language, use cases or models. Validation checks validity, consistency, completeness, realism and verifiability through reviews, prototypes and test-case generation. Management handles change with baselines, traceability and change control."},{"n":5,"name":"System Modeling","hook":"A model is a useful lie: it drops detail deliberately so one question can be answered clearly.","steps":[{"t":"Start at the boundary","d":"A context model fixes what is inside the system and which external systems and actors feed it."},{"t":"Then ask who wants what","d":"Use case diagrams show actors and goals across that boundary, including optional and extending behaviour."},{"t":"Then ask in what order","d":"Sequence diagrams place messages on a timeline and expose failure branches that a use case hides."},{"t":"Then ask what it is made of","d":"Class diagrams give entities, attributes, operations, associations, multiplicity and generalisation."},{"t":"Then ask how it reacts","d":"State machines model event-driven objects; activity diagrams model workflow, decisions and concurrency."},{"t":"Each view hides what the next needs","d":"That gap is why several consistent models are required, and why MDA tries to transform CIM to PIM to PSM."}],"keys":[{"k":"System model","v":"An abstract graphical or formal view of a system, usually expressed in UML."},{"k":"Context model","v":"Defines the system boundary and the external systems and actors it interacts with."},{"k":"Use case diagram","v":"Actors and the functional goals they pursue; includes and extends relationships."},{"k":"Sequence diagram","v":"Time-ordered messages between objects for one scenario, showing lifelines and returns."},{"k":"Class diagram","v":"Static structure: classes, attributes, operations, associations, multiplicity, generalisation, aggregation."},{"k":"State machine diagram","v":"States, events, guards and transitions for an object whose behaviour depends on history."},{"k":"Activity diagram","v":"Workflow of actions with decisions, forks and joins for concurrent flows."},{"k":"Generalisation","v":"Inheritance relationship where a subclass specialises a superclass and inherits its features."},{"k":"MDA layers","v":"CIM (business), PIM (platform independent), PSM (platform specific), related by transformations."},{"k":"Data-driven model","v":"Entity-relationship model describing data entities, attributes and relationships."}],"traps":["Drawing a class diagram when the question asks for interaction or behaviour.","Putting the payment gateway inside the system boundary instead of outside it.","Drawing use cases as functions rather than goals of a named actor.","Praising MDA without noting tooling cost and abstraction leakage."],"asks":["Draw a context model and use case diagram for an Online Food Delivery System.","Draw use case diagram, class diagram and sequence diagram for online book recommendation system.","Differentiate between structural and behavioral models. What is the importance of behavioral model?","What do you understand by model driven architecture? Explain.","Explain the behavioral model with example.","Discuss the importance of use case diagram in object-oriented development."],"skel":["Context first: boundary and external actors","Interaction: use case (who/what), sequence (order in time)","Structure: class diagram, multiplicity, generalisation","Behaviour: state machine (events), activity (workflow, concurrency)","Views must stay consistent with each other","MDA: CIM to PIM to PSM by transformation"],"answer":"Modelling starts by fixing the boundary. A context model for a library places the Library Management System at the centre with external actors and services around it: Member, Librarian, and the payment gateway and email service it depends on. A use case diagram then shows functional goals across that boundary: Borrow Book, Return Book, Reserve Book, Search Catalogue, Pay Fine, Manage Members. Member and Librarian are actors, and Pay Fine extends Return Book as an optional path. A class diagram gives the static structure: Member, Book, Copy, Loan, Reservation and Fine, with associativity and multiplicity — one Member has many Loans, one Loan refers to one Copy, one Book has many Copies — plus generalisation from Member to Student and Teacher. A sequence diagram fixes message order for one scenario, such as Borrow Book through the interface to LoanController and Catalogue, including the branch when no copy is available."},{"n":6,"name":"Architectural Design","hook":"Architecture is the set of decisions that are expensive to reverse, taken while information is still scarce.","steps":[{"t":"Non-functional requirements arrive as architecture","d":"Performance, security and availability cannot be bolted on later, so they constrain structure before code."},{"t":"So the architect commits early","d":"Decisions: system type, distribution, style, platform, data ownership, and buy versus build."},{"t":"Commitments are described in views","d":"Logical, process, development and physical views, joined by scenarios — the 4+1 model."},{"t":"Views are filled from known patterns","d":"Layered, client-server, MVC, pipe and filter, repository, service-oriented, event-driven."},{"t":"Every pattern buys one quality by selling another","d":"Layering buys substitutability and costs latency; a repository buys consistency and costs a bottleneck."},{"t":"Hence the paper asks for pros and cons","d":"Marks go to the trade-off and the diagram, not to a definition recited from memory."}],"keys":[{"k":"Architectural design","v":"Identifying overall structure: components, their properties and the relationships between them."},{"k":"4+1 views","v":"Logical, process, development, physical, plus scenarios that validate the set."},{"k":"Layered pattern","v":"Stacked layers each using the one below; substitutable, but adds latency and sinkhole layers."},{"k":"Client-server","v":"Clients request services from servers; central data and security, but a single point of failure."},{"k":"MVC","v":"Separates model, view and controller so the interface can change independently of data and logic."},{"k":"Pipe and filter","v":"Data flows through sequential transformations; reusable filters, poor for interactive dialogue."},{"k":"Repository pattern","v":"Components share one central store; consistent data, but the store becomes a bottleneck."},{"k":"Microservices","v":"Independently deployable services over standard protocols; scalable, but distributed complexity."},{"k":"Application architectures","v":"Data processing, transaction processing, event processing and language processing."},{"k":"Design decision","v":"A structural choice made early and costly to reverse, such as distribution or platform."}],"traps":["Describing a pattern without drawing it, when the question explicitly awards diagram marks.","Asserting one pattern is best instead of naming the quality it trades away.","Confusing MVC with client-server; MVC is a UI decomposition, not a deployment topology.","Forgetting application architecture types such as transaction or language processing."],"asks":["Explain architectural views. Illustrate layered, repository, and pipe and filter architecture.","Explain architectural design decisions that must be made during the architectural design process.","Explain any four architectural patterns with pros and cons.","Explain any one application of architecture.","Describe MVC, client-server and pipe and filter architecture with suitable diagrams.","What are the activities of architectural design process?"],"skel":["Architecture = components + relationships + properties","Decisions: type, distribution, style, platform, data, buy or build","Views: logical, process, development, physical, scenarios","Patterns: layered, client-server, MVC, pipe-filter, repository, services","Each pattern trades one quality attribute for another","Application types: data, transaction, event, language processing"],"answer":"Architectural design fixes the system's overall structure — components, their properties and relationships — and it matters because non-functional requirements are decided there. Performance, security, availability and maintainability cannot be retrofitted by better code, so the architect makes the expensive-to-reverse decisions: system type, distribution, architecture style, platform, data ownership and buy versus build. These are recorded in views: logical, process, development and physical, plus scenarios that tie them together, the 4+1 model. Patterns supply known solutions. Layered separates presentation, business and persistence, giving substitutability at the cost of latency and sinkhole layers. Client-server centralises data and security but adds a single point of failure. MVC separates model, view and controller so the interface changes independently. Pipe and filter composes transformations for reuse, but suits batch work rather than interactive dialogue. A repository gives consistent shared data at the cost of a bottleneck."},{"n":7,"name":"Design and Implementation","hook":"Design is the stage where future change is made either cheap or impossible.","steps":[{"t":"Requirements are decomposed into components","d":"Each component gets a clear responsibility and an interface, so work can proceed in parallel."},{"t":"Good decomposition has a measurable signature","d":"High cohesion inside, low coupling between, information hidden behind interfaces, guided by SOLID."},{"t":"Recurring problems get named solutions","d":"GoF patterns codify creational, structural and behavioural answers, giving design a shared vocabulary."},{"t":"Design meets reality in implementation","d":"Reuse of libraries, frameworks and COTS, host-target development, IDEs and coding standards shape the code."},{"t":"Reality includes other people editing the same files","d":"Configuration management supplies version control, reproducible builds, baselines and release control."},{"t":"Which is why implementation issues is a full question","d":"Reuse, configuration management and host-target each fail in their own way, and each needs its own mitigation."}],"keys":[{"k":"Cohesion","v":"Degree to which a component's responsibilities belong together; high cohesion is desirable."},{"k":"Coupling","v":"Degree of dependence between components; low coupling limits the ripple of change."},{"k":"Information hiding","v":"Exposing only stable interfaces and concealing internal representation and decisions."},{"k":"Singleton","v":"Creational pattern guaranteeing one instance with a global access point, e.g. a config registry."},{"k":"Adapter","v":"Structural pattern converting one interface into another that clients expect."},{"k":"Observer","v":"Behavioural pattern where subjects notify registered observers of state change, as in event listeners."},{"k":"Configuration management","v":"Version control, build management, change control and release management over defined baselines."},{"k":"Host-target development","v":"Developing on one platform and executing on another, requiring cross-compilers, simulators or containers."},{"k":"Open-source licence","v":"GPL imposes copyleft on derivatives; MIT and Apache permit proprietary reuse with attribution."},{"k":"Technical debt","v":"Short-term design compromises whose interest is paid as slower future change."}],"traps":["Naming a pattern without stating its intent and one concrete example.","Reversing cohesion and coupling, or claiming both should be high.","Reducing configuration management to version control alone.","Ignoring licensing differences when discussing reuse of open-source components."],"asks":["What do you understand by design patterns? What role does it have in object oriented design?","What are the issues that may occur while implementation? How can these issues be handled?","Explain the steps that are carried out during configuration management.","What are the drawbacks of software reuse? Explain.","Discuss reuse, configuration management, and host-target development as implementation issues.","Write short notes on open-source development."],"skel":["Design: components, interfaces, responsibilities","Principles: cohesion high, coupling low, information hiding, SOLID","GoF: creational, structural, behavioural — intent plus example","Implementation issues: reuse, configuration management, host-target","CM: versions, builds, baselines, change control, releases","Reuse risks: licence, vendor lock-in, integration, hidden defects"],"answer":"Design patterns are named, reusable solutions to recurring design problems within a context, and their value in object-oriented design is vocabulary plus flexibility: saying 'use an Observer here' communicates structure and expected change in two words. The GoF groups them three ways. Creational patterns control instantiation — Singleton for one shared instance, Factory Method to defer the concrete class, Builder for stepwise construction. Structural patterns compose objects — Adapter to reconcile incompatible interfaces, Decorator to add behaviour without subclassing, Facade to simplify a subsystem. Behavioural patterns distribute responsibility — Observer for publish-subscribe updates, Strategy to swap algorithms, Command to encapsulate requests, Template Method to fix a skeleton and vary steps. Each must be justified by the change it absorbs; applying a pattern without a force to resolve adds indirection and obscures otherwise simple code."},{"n":8,"name":"Software Testing","hook":"Testing cannot prove a program correct; it can only make the survival of a defect unlikely and its cost visible.","steps":[{"t":"Two different questions are being asked","d":"Verification: are we building the product right, against specification? Validation: are we building the right product for the user?"},{"t":"Some defects are cheaper to see than to run","d":"Inspection reviews code and design statically against checklists, catching classes of fault that execution masks."},{"t":"Execution then proceeds in levels","d":"Unit with stubs and drivers, integration across interfaces, system against requirements, acceptance by the customer."},{"t":"Each level needs deliberate test design","d":"Black-box: equivalence partitioning, boundary values, decision tables. White-box: statement, branch, path coverage."},{"t":"Coverage is a measure, not a target","d":"Full branch coverage still misses omitted requirements, timing faults and wrong assumptions."},{"t":"TDD inverts the order","d":"Red, green, refactor makes the test the specification, so design stays testable and regression is nearly free."}],"keys":[{"k":"Verification","v":"Does the product conform to its specification? A static, build-it-right question."},{"k":"Validation","v":"Does the product meet the user's real need? A dynamic, build-the-right-thing question."},{"k":"Software inspection","v":"Formal team review of code or design against a checklist, without executing it."},{"k":"Unit testing","v":"Isolated component testing using stubs and drivers, often white-box coverage-driven."},{"k":"Integration strategies","v":"Top-down with stubs, bottom-up with drivers, sandwich, or big-bang."},{"k":"System testing","v":"Whole system against requirements, including performance, load, security, usability and recovery."},{"k":"Release testing","v":"Black-box testing by the development team against requirements before handover."},{"k":"Alpha and beta testing","v":"Alpha is user testing at the developer's site; beta is released to real users in their own environment."},{"k":"Equivalence partitioning","v":"Dividing inputs into classes treated alike, then testing one representative per class."},{"k":"TDD cycle","v":"Red: failing test. Green: minimal code to pass. Refactor: clean up with tests as a safety net."}],"traps":["Using verification and validation interchangeably; they answer different questions.","Saying alpha testing is done by customers at their own site, which describes beta.","Equating one hundred per cent statement coverage with a correct system.","Listing test levels without saying what defect each level is designed to find."],"asks":["Differentiate between verification and validation. Explain levels of testing.","Explain the software testing process; differentiate development and release testing; how does TDD improve quality?","List different stakeholders involved in software inspection. How is software inspection carried out?","Explain the process of release testing and user testing.","Differentiate between Alpha testing and Beta testing.","Discuss path testing with suitable example."],"skel":["Verification: built right; validation: right product","Inspection is static, team-based, checklist-driven","Levels: unit, integration, system, acceptance","Black-box: partition, boundary, decision table, state","White-box: statement, branch, condition, path","TDD: red, green, refactor; test is the specification"],"answer":"Testing is dynamic verification: executing the system to expose defects. Verification asks whether the product conforms to its specification; validation asks whether it meets the user's real need. Neither proves correctness — testing can only show the presence of errors, so its value is early detection and measured confidence. The process is planned: test cases designed from requirements and design, data and environment prepared, tests executed, results compared with expectations, defects logged and fixed, then regression to confirm nothing broke, ending at defined exit criteria. Levels follow assembly order. Unit testing isolates components with stubs and drivers, using white-box coverage such as statement and branch. Integration testing checks interfaces top-down, bottom-up or sandwich. System testing checks the whole against requirements, including performance and security. Release testing is black-box testing by the development team before handover; acceptance testing, alpha in-house and beta with real users, is the customer's judgement."},{"n":9,"name":"Software Evolution","hook":"Software is never finished; the only choice is whether change stays cheap or becomes catastrophic.","steps":[{"t":"Delivery starts the cost curve, not ends it","d":"Roughly 60 per cent of lifetime effort is spent after the first release, on change."},{"t":"Change arrives from four directions","d":"Defects to fix, environments to adapt to, new requirements to satisfy, and decay to prevent."},{"t":"That gives maintenance its four names","d":"Corrective about 20, adaptive about 25, perfective about 50, preventive about 5 per cent."},{"t":"Old systems accumulate all four at once","d":"A legacy system couples application code, support software, hardware, data and business process."},{"t":"So assess before acting","d":"Plot business value against system quality: maintain, scrap and replace, re-engineer, wrap behind an API, or phase out."},{"t":"Lehman explains why it never ends","d":"Continuing change and increasing complexity mean effort must go into structure, or quality declines steadily."}],"keys":[{"k":"Software evolution","v":"Continuous change of a delivered system to meet new needs, fix defects and adapt to environment."},{"k":"Corrective maintenance","v":"Fixing reported defects and failures; about 20 per cent of maintenance effort."},{"k":"Adaptive maintenance","v":"Adapting to new platforms, environments or regulations; about 25 per cent."},{"k":"Perfective maintenance","v":"Adding or improving functionality, performance and maintainability; about 50 per cent, the largest share."},{"k":"Preventive maintenance","v":"Refactoring and restructuring to slow future deterioration; about 5 per cent."},{"k":"Legacy system","v":"Old system still performing essential work, coupling hardware, support software, application, data and process."},{"k":"Re-engineering","v":"Restructuring and modernising a system while preserving its functionality, often after reverse engineering."},{"k":"Reverse engineering","v":"Recovering design and specification from existing code, without changing it."},{"k":"Wrapping","v":"Giving a legacy system a new interface or API so other systems can use it unchanged."},{"k":"Lehman's laws","v":"Continuing change, increasing complexity, conservation of familiarity, declining quality without maintenance."}],"traps":["Naming perfective maintenance as the smallest category; it is the largest at about half.","Confusing re-engineering with reverse engineering: one changes the system, the other only recovers design.","Defining a legacy system as merely old rather than business-critical and costly to change.","Describing evolution as bug fixing only, ignoring adaptation and new features."],"asks":["Define software maintenance. Explain the software maintenance process.","Differentiate between corrective, adaptive, perfective, and preventive maintenance. Which consumes the most resources?","What do you mean by legacy system? Explain its importance.","Differentiate between reengineering and reverse engineering.","Why do you think software needs to be changed? Explain a model to cope with change.","Software maintenance is one of the most important activities. Justify with an example."],"skel":["Evolution continues after delivery; about 60 per cent of cost","Corrective 20, adaptive 25, perfective 50, preventive 5","Perfective dominates because users keep asking for more","Legacy = code + support software + hardware + data + process","Choose: maintain, scrap, re-engineer, wrap, phase out","Lehman: change continues, complexity grows without effort"],"answer":"Software evolution is the change made to a system after delivery, and it dominates cost: roughly 60 per cent of lifetime effort is post-delivery, so maintainability decides total cost. Change arrives in four kinds, which name the maintenance types. Corrective maintenance fixes reported defects, about 20 per cent. Adaptive maintenance responds to a new environment such as platform, regulation or hardware, about 25 per cent. Perfective maintenance adds or improves functionality and performance, about 50 per cent and therefore the largest consumer of effort, because users keep asking for more. Preventive maintenance refactors to slow future decay, about 5 per cent. Legacy systems concentrate all four: application software, support software, hardware, data and business processes are coupled, so change is risky and expensive. Assessment plots business value against system quality to choose maintain, scrap and replace, re-engineer, wrap behind an API, or phase out."},{"n":10,"name":"Software Management","hook":"Estimation is guessing with arithmetic — but a documented guess is a commitment you can defend.","steps":[{"t":"Scope has to become tasks","d":"A work breakdown structure decomposes the product into units that can be estimated, assigned and tracked."},{"t":"Then size becomes effort","d":"Expert judgement, analogy, function points, story points or algorithmic models, each trading accuracy for cost."},{"t":"COCOMO makes the arithmetic explicit","d":"Effort = a(KLOC)^b and time = c(Effort)^d, with organic 2.4/1.05, semi-detached 3.0/1.12, embedded 3.6/1.20."},{"t":"Effort becomes a schedule","d":"Gantt charts, milestones, critical path and resource levelling turn person-months into dates."},{"t":"Schedule meets uncertainty as risk","d":"Identify, analyse by probability times impact, plan to avoid, reduce, transfer or accept, then monitor."},{"t":"People and configuration keep it stable","d":"Motivation, team structure, quality assurance and version control are why the paper asks management broadly."}],"keys":[{"k":"Project management","v":"Planning, monitoring and controlling people, process and product to meet time, cost and quality."},{"k":"Work breakdown structure","v":"Hierarchical decomposition of project work into estimable, assignable tasks."},{"k":"COCOMO","v":"Constructive Cost Model: algorithmic estimation from size in KLOC and project mode."},{"k":"COCOMO modes","v":"Organic (small, familiar), semi-detached (medium), embedded (large, tight constraints)."},{"k":"COCOMO equations","v":"Effort = a(KLOC)^b person-months; time = c(Effort)^d months; team = effort divided by time."},{"k":"Function points","v":"Size measure from inputs, outputs, queries, files and interfaces, independent of language."},{"k":"Risk exposure","v":"Probability of the risk multiplied by the loss if it occurs, used to rank risks."},{"k":"Risk strategies","v":"Avoid, reduce, transfer and accept, with contingency plans for the accepted ones."},{"k":"Quality assurance vs control","v":"Assurance defines the process that should produce quality; control tests and reviews the product."},{"k":"Configuration management","v":"Version control, build management, change control and release management over baselines."}],"traps":["Forgetting that COCOMO effort is in person-months and duration in months.","Substituting effort for time in the formula c(Effort)^d.","Listing risks with no probability, impact or mitigation.","Treating quality control as identical to quality assurance."],"asks":["List some advantages of estimation. Explain COCOMO model.","A project is 320 KLOC. Using COCOMO, calculate effort and development time for organic and embedded modes.","What is project management? Explain different project management activities in detail.","How is risk management carried out during software development? Explain.","What is software quality management? Explain quality management, assurance and control with examples.","Explain the importance of software pricing. Highlight COCOMO cost modelling."],"skel":["Plan: WBS, estimate, schedule, resources, risk, quality","Estimate by judgement, analogy, function points, COCOMO","COCOMO: E = a(KLOC)^b, T = c(E)^d","Organic 2.4/1.05, semi 3.0/1.12, embedded 3.6/1.20","Schedule: Gantt, milestones, critical path","Risk: identify, analyse, plan, monitor"],"answer":"Project management plans, monitors and controls people, process and product so the system is delivered on time, within budget and at the required quality. Planning starts with scope and a work breakdown structure, which turns the product into estimable tasks. Effort follows from size, by expert judgement, analogy, function points or an algorithmic model. COCOMO computes effort as a(KLOC)^b and duration as c(Effort)^d, with organic, semi-detached and embedded modes: for 320 KLOC organic, effort is 2.4 times 320 to the power 1.05, giving roughly 1,025 person-months, and time is 2.5 times that effort to the power 0.38; embedded uses 3.6 and 1.20 instead, and gives a larger effort over a similar duration. Effort becomes a schedule through Gantt charts, milestones and the critical path. Uncertainty is handled as risk: identify, analyse by probability and impact, plan to avoid, reduce, transfer or accept, then monitor."}],"cdc":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to Compiler","hook":"A compiler is not one translator but six narrow ones, each with an input and output you can draw, test and mark.","steps":[{"t":"Characters become tokens","d":"The lexical analyser scans the source, strips whitespace and comments, groups characters into lexemes and returns tokens like <id,1>, filling the symbol table."},{"t":"Tokens become a tree","d":"The syntax analyser matches the token stream against the context-free grammar and builds a parse tree, reporting syntax errors with recovery."},{"t":"The tree gets meaning","d":"The semantic analyser resolves names, checks types and annotates the tree, catching int x = \"s\" and inserting coercions; no code is produced here."},{"t":"The tree is lowered","d":"The intermediate code generator flattens the annotated tree into machine-independent three-address code, ending the analysis (front-end) half."},{"t":"The IR is improved, then mapped","d":"The optimiser shrinks the IR and the code generator chooses registers and instructions, completing the machine-dependent synthesis (back-end) half."},{"t":"Two tables cut across all six","d":"The symbol table and error handler are consulted by every phase, so the block diagram draws them as vertical boxes, not as phases."}],"keys":[{"k":"Compiler","v":"Translates a whole source program into an equivalent target program before execution, reporting errors and producing object code."},{"k":"Interpreter","v":"Executes source statement by statement, produces no object file, and stops at the first error."},{"k":"Analysis (front end)","v":"Lexical, syntax, semantic analysis plus intermediate code generation; language dependent, ends in machine-independent IR."},{"k":"Synthesis (back end)","v":"Optimisation and code generation; machine dependent, turns IR into target instructions."},{"k":"Token","v":"A pair <token-name, attribute-value> such as <id,1> or <if>; the lexer's output unit."},{"k":"Symbol table","v":"Shared table of identifier attributes, created by the lexer and read by every later phase."},{"k":"Error handler","v":"Routine each phase calls on an error so compilation continues and more errors are reported."},{"k":"One-pass compiler","v":"Scans the source once with no explicit IR; fast, but weak optimisation and no forward references."},{"k":"Preprocessor","v":"Expands macros, includes files and evaluates conditional compilation before the compiler proper sees the source."},{"k":"Retargeting","v":"One front end with several back ends, made possible because the IR hides the machine."}],"traps":["Saying semantic analysis generates code; it only annotates and checks types.","Putting intermediate code generation in the back end; it is the last front-end phase.","Drawing the symbol table as a phase box instead of a table spanning all phases.","Confusing fast execution (compiled code) with fast translation (interpreters start sooner).","Naming the parser as the phase that strips comments; that is the lexer's job."],"asks":["Explain briefly about different phases involved in compiler, with a block diagram.","Simulate execution in the different phases of a compiler for the statement A * B + C - 2.5.","Differentiate between one-pass and multi-pass compiler.","Difference between compiler and interpreter.","Symbol table is a necessary component of compiler. Justify this statement.","Discuss the importance of error handler in compiler and how it is used in different phases."],"skel":["Lexer: characters to tokens, symbol table entries, illegal-character errors","Parser: tokens to parse tree over the CFG, syntax errors","Semantic: scope resolution, type checking, annotated tree, coercion insertion","ICG: annotated tree to three-address code, machine independent","Optimiser then code generator: IR to registers to assembly","Symbol table and error handler span all six phases"],"answer":"A compiler translates a source program into an equivalent target program through six phases. The lexical analyser scans characters, groups them into lexemes and returns tokens such as <id,1>, enters identifiers in the symbol table and deletes whitespace and comments. The syntax analyser checks the token stream against the context-free grammar and builds a parse tree. The semantic analyser resolves names, checks types, annotates the tree and reports mismatches. The intermediate code generator lowers that tree to machine-independent three-address code. The optimiser improves the IR, then the code generator maps it onto registers and target instructions. The first three phases plus intermediate code generation form the language-dependent front end, called analysis; optimisation and code generation form the machine-dependent back end, called synthesis. The symbol table and error handler are not phases: both are consulted by all six, so the block diagram draws them as vertical boxes."},{"n":2,"name":"Lexical Analysis and Syntax Analysis","hook":"Every scanner is a DFA built from a regular expression, and every parse table is a machine built from a grammar; both are mechanical, so both are bankable marks.","steps":[{"t":"Fix the token vocabulary","d":"The lexer must return <token-name, attribute-value> for each lexeme, so each token needs a pattern written as a regular expression."},{"t":"Regex becomes an ε-NFA","d":"Thompson's construction builds a fragment per operator: symbols are two states, concatenation chains them, union and star add ε-transitions."},{"t":"ε-NFA becomes a DFA","d":"Subset construction makes each DFA state the ε-closure of a set of NFA states, computing ε-closure(move(T,a)) for every symbol until no new set appears."},{"t":"The DFA is minimised and coded","d":"Partition final and non-final states, split groups that disagree on a transition, merge the stable partition, then emit a table-driven scanner or a LEX rule set."},{"t":"Tokens meet a grammar","d":"The parser needs a CFG; left recursion, left factoring and ambiguity are removed first because predictive parsing cannot handle them."},{"t":"Direction chooses the table","d":"Top-down needs FIRST and FOLLOW for an LL(1) table; bottom-up needs items, closure and goto for an LR table, and lookahead decides SLR, CLR or LALR."}],"keys":[{"k":"Token, lexeme, pattern","v":"Token is the <name, value> pair returned; lexeme is the matched characters; pattern is the regex describing lexemes."},{"k":"Thompson's construction","v":"Compositional regex-to-ε-NFA build: base symbol, concatenation, union and star each get a fixed fragment with ε-moves."},{"k":"Subset construction","v":"DFA state is a set of NFA states; next state is ε-closure(move(T,a)). Accepting sets contain the NFA final state."},{"k":"DFA minimisation","v":"Split into final and non-final groups, refine groups whose members move to different groups, repeat until stable."},{"k":"FIRST(α)","v":"Terminals that can begin a string derived from α; contains ε if α derives ε."},{"k":"FOLLOW(A)","v":"Terminals that may appear immediately right of A; contains $ when A is the start symbol."},{"k":"LL(1) condition","v":"Every table cell M[A,a] holds at most one production; a grammar with a double entry is not LL(1)."},{"k":"Left recursion removal","v":"A → Aα | β becomes A → βA′ and A′ → αA′ | ε, keeping the same language."},{"k":"LR(0) item","v":"A production with a dot showing how much of the right side is already on the stack."},{"k":"Handle","v":"The right-hand side matching the stack top that a reduce step replaces by its left-hand side."}],"traps":["Skipping ε-closure after each move in subset construction, so DFA states are missing.","Omitting $ from FOLLOW of the start symbol, or adding $ to every non-terminal.","Removing left recursion without creating A′ and its ε production.","Calling a grammar LL(1) when one cell holds both a shift and a reduce.","Confusing a handle with the dot position in an item."],"asks":["Convert the regular expression (a + b)*a(a + b) to DFA.","Compute the FIRST and FOLLOW of the non-terminals in the following grammar.","Construct the LL(1) parsing table for the given grammar and show whether it is LL(1).","What is ambiguous grammar? Explain the dangling else problem and show how to resolve it.","Compute the LR(0) item set for the grammar S → aSa | bSb | d.","List some limitations of recursive descent parsing."],"skel":["Token vocabulary as regular expressions","Thompson: regex to ε-NFA with ε-transitions","Subset construction: ε-closure sets become DFA states","Minimise, then emit scanner table or LEX rules","Grammar hygiene: remove ambiguity, left recursion, left factor","LL(1) via FIRST/FOLLOW, LR via items, closure, goto"],"answer":"Take (a|b)*abb. Thompson's construction builds the ε-NFA compositionally: a and b are two-state fragments, the union joins them with a new start and new final state through ε-moves, the star adds a loop with ε-shortcuts past the fragment, and concatenation joins one fragment's final state to the next start with an ε. Because it has ε-transitions it cannot run directly, so subset construction determinises it. Start with ε-closure of the start state. For each DFA state T and each input symbol a, compute ε-closure(move(T,a)); if that set is new, it becomes a new DFA state. Each DFA state is a set of NFA states, final if the set contains the NFA final state. Minimise by partitioning into final and non-final states, then splitting any group whose members move to different groups, until stable. The resulting table plus a case on final states is the scanner LEX generates."},{"n":3,"name":"Syntax-Directed Translation","hook":"A grammar says what a program looks like; an SDD attaches rules to the productions so the parser computes what it means while it parses.","steps":[{"t":"Attach rules to productions","d":"Each production gets semantic rules that define attributes of its non-terminals, turning a CFG into a syntax-directed definition."},{"t":"Classify the attributes","d":"A synthesized attribute is computed from its children, so it flows up; an inherited attribute comes from the parent or a left sibling, so it flows down or across."},{"t":"Restrict to get a single pass","d":"If only synthesized attributes are used the definition is S-attributed and one post-order traversal evaluates it, because children are done before parents."},{"t":"Loosen it for top-down","d":"L-attributed allows inherited attributes supplied only by the parent and left siblings, so one left-to-right depth-first pass works with a predictive parser."},{"t":"Write the values on the tree","d":"The annotated parse tree shows every attribute value at every node; this drawing, not prose, is what earns the marks."},{"t":"Condense it when sharing","d":"A syntax tree keeps operators as interior nodes and operands as leaves; a DAG shares common subexpressions, so evaluation order needs a topological sort of the dependency graph."}],"keys":[{"k":"Syntax-directed definition","v":"A CFG where each production carries semantic rules computing attribute values for its symbols."},{"k":"Synthesized attribute","v":"Value computed from the attribute values of the node's children; information flows upward."},{"k":"Inherited attribute","v":"Value taken from the parent node or a left sibling; information flows downward or sideways."},{"k":"S-attributed definition","v":"Uses only synthesized attributes, so one bottom-up post-order traversal evaluates every value."},{"k":"L-attributed definition","v":"Inherited attributes depend only on the parent and left siblings, allowing one left-to-right pass."},{"k":"Annotated parse tree","v":"A parse tree with each attribute's value written beside the node that owns it."},{"k":"Syntax tree","v":"Condensed parse tree with operators as interior nodes and operands as leaves; grammar-only nodes removed."},{"k":"DAG","v":"Syntax tree in which repeated subexpressions are one shared node, so the value is computed once."},{"k":"Dependency graph","v":"Nodes are attributes, edges are dependencies; a topological sort gives a legal evaluation order."},{"k":"mknode, mkleaf, mkop","v":"Functions that build syntax tree and DAG nodes for interior operators, leaves and labelled operators."}],"traps":["Claiming L-attributed permits any inherited attribute; only parent and left-sibling sources are allowed.","Evaluating an S-attributed definition top-down, before child values exist.","Drawing a syntax tree that still contains every grammar non-terminal, which is a parse tree.","Forgetting that a DAG node with two parents means one computation of a shared subexpression."],"asks":["Define syntax directed definition and construct an annotated parse tree for (5*3+2)*5.","Differentiate between S-attributed and L-attributed definitions with examples.","Construct a syntax directed translation scheme translating infix to postfix; annotate 9 - 5 + 2.","Define inherited and synthesized attribute with an example of each.","What is an annotated parse tree? Define S-attributed grammar with an example.","Distinguish between DAG and syntax tree."],"skel":["SDD: CFG plus semantic rules per production","Synthesized flows up, inherited flows down or across","S-attributed: post-order, one pass","L-attributed: left-to-right, works top-down","Annotated parse tree carries the marks","Syntax tree condenses, DAG shares subexpressions"],"answer":"A syntax-directed definition attaches semantic rules to each production, so the parser computes meaning while it parses. For infix to postfix one synthesized attribute code suffices: E → E1 + T {E.code = E1.code || T.code || '+'}, E → T {E.code = T.code}, T → T1 * F {T.code = T1.code || F.code || '*'}, T → F {T.code = F.code}, F → (E) {F.code = E.code}, F → digit {F.code = digit.lexval}. Every attribute is synthesized, so the definition is S-attributed and a single post-order traversal evaluates it: children before parent. For 9 - 5 + 2 the leaves carry 9, 5 and 2; F and T pass them up unchanged; the inner E → E + T combines 9 and 5 into 95-, and the outer E gives 95-2+. The annotated parse tree writes these strings at each node bottom-up, which is what the paper marks."},{"n":4,"name":"Type Checking","hook":"A program can parse perfectly and still be nonsense, so the compiler needs rules that give every expression a type and reject the ones that cannot run.","steps":[{"t":"A grammar is not enough","d":"x = \"a\" + 1 satisfies the grammar, so a type system must assign types to expressions and enforce consistency between them."},{"t":"Types are built by constructors","d":"Represent a type as a type expression: int, array(2,int), pointer(int), record(field:type), function; the constructor tree is the type."},{"t":"Infer with syntax-directed rules","d":"Attach one rule per production, so E → E1 + E2 yields int when both children are int and float when either is float."},{"t":"Look names up, then complain","d":"Identifiers get their type from the symbol table; a mismatch raises a type error, and a legal mismatch triggers an inserted coercion."},{"t":"Convert only where it is safe","d":"Widening int to float to double is implicit and lossless; narrowing double to int needs an explicit cast because information is lost."},{"t":"Decide what same type means","d":"Structural equivalence compares shape, name equivalence compares declared names, and that choice decides whether an assignment is accepted."}],"keys":[{"k":"Type system","v":"Set of rules assigning a type to every expression and enforcing consistency between them."},{"k":"Static checking","v":"Type errors found at compile time, as in C and Java; dynamic checking happens at run time, as in Python."},{"k":"Type expression","v":"A type built by constructors: int, array(2,int), pointer(int), record, function."},{"k":"Type inference","v":"Deducing an expression's type from its subexpressions' types using the SDD rules for each production."},{"k":"Widening","v":"Implicit, safe promotion that loses no information: int to float to double."},{"k":"Narrowing","v":"Explicit cast that may lose information: double to int."},{"k":"Coercion","v":"Conversion inserted silently by the compiler; a cast is written by the programmer."},{"k":"Structural equivalence","v":"Two types are equal when their structures match; name equivalence requires the same declared type name."},{"k":"Polymorphism","v":"One name, several types: parametric (generics), ad-hoc (overloading), subtype (inheritance)."}],"traps":["Calling widening a cast; the compiler inserts it without the programmer writing anything.","Checking types after code generation, when the IR has already lost the source types.","Treating array(2,int) and array(3,int) as structurally equivalent; the bound is part of the type.","Omitting function arity and parameter types from the checker's rules."],"asks":["Why do we need to check the type?","Differentiate between static and dynamic type checking with examples.","What is a type expression? List the kinds of type expression.","How can we carry out type checking for the given expression using a syntax-directed definition?","Discuss about type conversion.","Define a type checking system with examples."],"skel":["Type system assigns and enforces types","Type expressions built by constructors","SDD rules infer types bottom-up","Symbol table supplies identifier types","Widening implicit, narrowing needs a cast","Equivalence: structural versus name"],"answer":"A type system is the set of rules giving every expression a type and rejecting inconsistent ones. Checking is needed because a syntactically valid program can still be meaningless: x = \"a\" + 1 parses but cannot execute, and catching it at compile time is cheaper than crashing at run time. Types are type expressions built by constructors such as int, array(2,int), pointer(int) and function. A simple checker is a syntax-directed definition. For E → E1 + E2, if both children are int then E is int; if either is float then E is float with a widening coercion inserted on the other; otherwise E.type is type_error. E → id takes its type from the symbol table, and E → num is int. Narrowing, float to int, is never inserted silently and needs an explicit cast. Static checking runs at compile time (C, Java), dynamic checking at run time (Python)."},{"n":5,"name":"Symbol Table Design","hook":"Every phase asks the same question about a name, so one shared table answers it once; the exam then tests how fast that answer is.","steps":[{"t":"One table, six clients","d":"The lexer creates entries, the parser adds structure, the semantic analyser adds types, and code generation reads offsets, so the table must be shared."},{"t":"Decide the attributes","d":"An entry holds name, kind, type, scope level, storage class and offset, size, declaration line, parameter list, return type and array bounds."},{"t":"Fix a tiny interface","d":"insert(name, attributes) on declaration, lookup(name) on use, and update or delete when a scope is left; everything else is representation."},{"t":"Price each structure","d":"Linear list is O(n) lookup but O(1) insert; sorted list searches in O(log n) yet inserts in O(n); a BST averages O(log n) and can degenerate."},{"t":"Choose hashing, then face collisions","d":"A hash table gives O(1) average insert and lookup, so the real question becomes the hash function and what happens when two names collide."},{"t":"Handle collisions and scopes","d":"Resolve by open addressing or chaining, push a new table per block and pop on exit so an inner declaration shadows an outer one."}],"keys":[{"k":"Symbol table","v":"Data structure storing each identifier with its attributes, consulted by every phase of the compiler."},{"k":"Stored attributes","v":"Name, kind, type, scope level, storage class and offset, size, line, parameter list, return type."},{"k":"Operations","v":"insert on declaration, lookup on use, update, and delete or undo at scope exit."},{"k":"Linear list","v":"O(n) lookup with O(1) insertion; acceptable only for very small tables."},{"k":"Hash table","v":"O(1) average insert and lookup; the standard choice in production compilers."},{"k":"Hash functions","v":"Division (key mod m), mid-square, and folding of the key's parts."},{"k":"Open addressing","v":"Collisions resolved by probing the table itself: linear, quadratic or double hashing."},{"k":"Chaining","v":"Each bucket holds a linked list, so colliding entries are simply appended to it."},{"k":"Scope stack","v":"A table per block, pushed on entry and popped on exit, giving shadowing for free."},{"k":"Load factor","v":"Entries divided by slots; when it rises, probes lengthen and the table is rehashed."}],"traps":["Claiming hashing is O(1) worst case; it is O(1) average and O(n) worst.","Popping nothing at scope exit, so an inner declaration leaks into the outer scope.","Giving insertion into a sorted list the same O(log n) cost as searching it.","Mixing open addressing and chaining inside one worked hash example."],"asks":["Explain the types of data structures used in a symbol table.","What are the typical entries made in a symbol table? Explain.","Compare linear list, hash table and binary search tree; which is most efficient and why?","Show hash table implementation for five identifiers using sum of ASCII mod 7 with chaining.","Symbol table is a necessary component of compiler. Justify with examples.","What types of information are provided by the symbol table?"],"skel":["Shared table, six client phases","Attributes: name, kind, type, scope, offset, size","Operations: insert, lookup, update, delete","Costs: linear O(n), sorted O(log n) search","Hash O(1) average, so collisions dominate","Chaining or probing; stack of tables per scope"],"answer":"The symbol table records every identifier with its attributes, namely name, kind, type, scope level, storage class and offset, size, declaration line, parameter list, return type and array bounds. Every phase consults it, which is why no compiler can do without it: the lexer inserts names, the semantic analyser fills in types, the code generator reads offsets. The interface is deliberately small: insert on declaration, lookup on use, and delete or undo at scope exit. Structures trade lookup against insertion. A linear list needs O(n) lookup but O(1) insertion and suits only tiny tables. A sorted list searches in O(log n) but inserts in O(n). A binary search tree averages O(log n) and degenerates to O(n). Hashing gives O(1) average for both and is what real compilers use, with collisions handled by open addressing or chaining, and scopes handled as a stack of tables so inner declarations shadow outer ones."},{"n":6,"name":"Run-time Storage Management","hook":"Every name needs an address while the program runs; the only real question is when that address is decided, and the answer is static, stack or heap.","steps":[{"t":"Allocation is a timing decision","d":"A name's storage can be fixed before execution, created on each call, or created on demand, so the compiler must know which lifetime applies."},{"t":"Static buys speed, forbids recursion","d":"Addresses fixed at compile time need sizes known then and no recursion, but cost nothing at run time."},{"t":"Stack buys recursion","d":"Each call pushes one activation record and each return pops it, so a recursive procedure simply has several frames alive at once."},{"t":"The layout fixes addressing","d":"Parameters, links, saved state, locals and temporaries sit at fixed offsets, so a name compiles to a (level, offset) pair."},{"t":"Links answer the hard question","d":"The control link reaches the caller for return; the access link reaches the enclosing lexical scope, which is how non-local names are found."},{"t":"Heap buys flexibility, costs safety","d":"malloc and new outlive the call, so storage is freed explicitly or by a collector, and the price is fragmentation, dangling pointers and garbage."}],"keys":[{"k":"Static allocation","v":"Addresses fixed at compile time; no recursion and all sizes must be known then."},{"k":"Stack allocation","v":"One activation record pushed per call and popped per return; supports recursion and local names."},{"k":"Heap allocation","v":"Storage requested at run time with malloc or new, outliving the call; needs explicit free or garbage collection."},{"k":"Activation record","v":"Return value, actual parameters, control and access links, saved machine status, local data, temporaries."},{"k":"Control (dynamic) link","v":"Points to the caller's activation record, used to restore state on return."},{"k":"Access (static) link","v":"Points to the frame of the enclosing lexical scope, used to reach non-local names."},{"k":"Activation tree","v":"Tree of activation records with one node per call; its depth is the recursion depth."},{"k":"Call by value","v":"The argument is copied in; by reference passes the address; by value-result copies in and back."},{"k":"Dangling pointer","v":"A reference to storage that has already been deallocated."},{"k":"Garbage and fragmentation","v":"Garbage is unreachable heap storage; fragmentation is free space split into unusable pieces."}],"traps":["Swapping the links: access is lexical, control is the caller.","Saying stack allocation supports recursion without one frame per call.","Claiming mark-and-sweep compacts storage; only copying collectors move objects.","Describing garbage collection as a compile-time activity."],"asks":["Draw and explain the complete structure of an activation record with all its components.","What is an activation record? Discuss the activities of caller and callee during call and return.","How do you represent recursion in an activation tree?","List out the different types of run-time storage management techniques.","What is an activation tree? Show it for a recursive factorial call.","Differentiate between static and dynamic link."],"skel":["Three lifetimes: static, stack, heap","Static fixed, no recursion, size known","Stack frame per call allows recursion","Record: params, links, status, locals, temps","Access link lexical, control link caller","Heap: fragmentation, dangling pointers, GC"],"answer":"Storage allocation decides when a name receives an address. Static allocation fixes addresses at compile time and therefore forbids recursion. Stack allocation pushes one activation record per call and pops it on return, which is what makes recursion work; heap allocation covers data that outlives the call. An activation record holds the actual parameters, the return value, the control link to the caller's frame, the access link to the frame of the enclosing lexical scope, saved machine status and registers, local data and temporaries. On a call the caller evaluates and passes arguments, saves the return address and old stack pointer; the callee saves registers and the old frame pointer, sets its own frame pointer and allocates locals. On return the callee restores registers and stack pointer and the caller pops the arguments. Non-local names are reached by following the access link as many levels as the nesting difference."},{"n":7,"name":"Intermediate Code Generation","hook":"A parse tree is too grammar-bound to optimise or retarget, so the compiler lowers it into instructions with one operator each — and that form is the most predictable question on the paper.","steps":[{"t":"Why lower at all","d":"The tree encodes grammar decisions that optimisers and back ends do not want, so it is rewritten into a machine-independent intermediate representation."},{"t":"Pick a level","d":"High-level IR keeps structure as trees and DAGs; low-level IR flattens to three-address code, quadruples, triples or indirect triples."},{"t":"One operator per instruction","d":"Three-address code allows at most one operator on the right, so a*b + c*d becomes two multiply instructions into temporaries and one add."},{"t":"Choose a representation","d":"Quadruples name the result, triples use the instruction's index as the result and save a temporary, indirect triples add a pointer array so code can be reordered."},{"t":"Control flow needs labels","d":"Boolean expressions and if/while translate into conditional and unconditional jumps, so the generator emits labels and gotos rather than expressions."},{"t":"Targets are unknown, so patch later","d":"Jump targets are not known when the jump is emitted, so truelist and falselist are merged and filled by backpatching once the target label exists."}],"keys":[{"k":"Intermediate representation","v":"Machine-independent form between front and back end; enables optimisation and retargeting."},{"k":"Three-address code","v":"Instruction with at most one operator, such as x = y + z, using compiler-generated temporaries."},{"k":"Quadruple","v":"(op, arg1, arg2, result); the result is named explicitly, so instructions can be moved."},{"k":"Triple","v":"(op, arg1, arg2) where the result is the triple's own position; saves temporaries but resists reordering."},{"k":"Indirect triple","v":"Triples stored in one array with a separate array of pointers, so order can change without renumbering."},{"k":"DAG","v":"Shares common subexpressions inside a block, so b * -c is computed once, not twice."},{"k":"truelist and falselist","v":"Lists of jump instructions awaiting the label to which a boolean expression transfers control."},{"k":"makelist(i)","v":"Creates a one-element list containing the index of instruction i."},{"k":"merge(p1, p2)","v":"Concatenates two backpatch lists into one."},{"k":"backpatch(p, target)","v":"Fills the label target into the target field of every jump in list p."}],"traps":["Recomputing -c twice when the DAG shares it; state t3 = t1 explicitly.","Writing x = y + z * w as one three-address instruction with two operators.","Giving a triple a named result instead of the instruction index.","Backpatching a list before merging the lists that belong to it."],"asks":["Convert the following expression to three-address code.","Generate three-address code, quadruples, triples and indirect triples for x = a*b + c*d - e.","What are the significances of intermediate code?","Explain with examples the different methods of intermediate code representation.","What is backpatching? Why is it used? Generate three-address code with backpatching.","Define three-address code and write the quadruples for a = -b*(c+d)/e."],"skel":["IR hides the machine, enables optimisation","High-level trees versus low-level 3AC","One operator per instruction, temporaries","Quadruples name results, triples use indices","Jumps need labels for if and while","Backpatch: makelist, merge, backpatch"],"answer":"Intermediate code is the machine-independent form between front and back ends; it enables optimisation and retargeting, and is easier than machine code. Three-address code allows at most one operator per instruction, using compiler temporaries. For x = a*b + c*d - e: t1 = a * b, t2 = c * d, t3 = t1 + t2, x = t3 - e. Quadruples name the result explicitly, so (*, a, b, t1), (*, c, d, t2), (+, t1, t2, t3) and (-, t3, e, x). Triples drop the result field and use the instruction's position, giving (0)(*,a,b), (1)(*,c,d), (2)(+,(0),(1)), (3)(-,(2),e). Indirect triples keep the triples in one array and hold an ordered array of pointers to them, so instructions can be reordered without rewriting operands. A DAG would share a repeated subexpression such as a*b, emitting t1 once. For control flow, boolean expressions produce truelist and falselist, filled by backpatching."},{"n":8,"name":"Code Generation","hook":"The IR is still machine independent; this unit is where abstract temporaries become real registers, and where register pressure becomes visible.","steps":[{"t":"The back end decides by machine","d":"Instruction selection depends on the target's instruction set, registers, addressing modes and memory hierarchy, so the same IR yields different code per machine."},{"t":"Cut the IR at every branch","d":"Leaders are the first instruction, any jump target and any instruction after a jump; each leader starts a basic block running to the next leader."},{"t":"Blocks become a graph","d":"Each block is a node and each possible transfer an edge, so loops appear as back edges and unreachable blocks stand alone."},{"t":"Compress each block","d":"A DAG inside a block removes common subexpressions and dead assignments and exposes dependencies before any instruction is chosen."},{"t":"Assign registers, then spill","d":"Live ranges are coloured with k colours for k registers; when the graph cannot be coloured, a value is spilled to memory and reloaded."},{"t":"Emit with descriptors","d":"A simple generator uses getReg() with register and address descriptors, and picks instructions by cost, as the dynamic programming algorithm does bottom-up."}],"keys":[{"k":"Basic block","v":"Maximal straight-line instruction sequence with a single entry and a single exit."},{"k":"Leader","v":"The first instruction, the target of a jump, or the instruction immediately following a jump."},{"k":"Flow graph","v":"Directed graph whose nodes are basic blocks and edges are possible transfers of control."},{"k":"DAG of a block","v":"Shows common subexpressions, dead code and dependencies within one basic block."},{"k":"Register allocation","v":"Mapping live ranges to a limited set of registers, usually by graph colouring with k colours."},{"k":"Spilling","v":"Moving a value to memory when live ranges outnumber available registers, then reloading it."},{"k":"getReg()","v":"Chooses a register for an operand using register descriptors and address descriptors."},{"k":"Peephole optimisation","v":"Local rewriting of target code over a small window of adjacent instructions."},{"k":"Dynamic programming generation","v":"Bottom-up minimum-cost instruction selection per tree node using cost vectors; optimal but exponential in registers."},{"k":"Next-use information","v":"Where a variable's value is next needed; drives register choice and spilling decisions."}],"traps":["Missing the instruction after a jump as a leader, which produces wrong blocks.","Treating an if-body as a single basic block; the branch splits it.","Allocating registers without spilling when live ranges exceed the register count.","Calling dynamic programming code generation linear; it is exponential in registers."],"asks":["How do you create a control flow graph from a basic block?","How do you recognize a basic block? Discuss the factors that affect code generation.","Construct the flow graph for the given code and identify all basic blocks.","What is register allocation? Explain it using the graph colouring algorithm.","Discuss the different factors affecting target code generation.","Write the code generation algorithm for the instruction a = b op c."],"skel":["Factors: instruction set, registers, addressing modes","Leaders: first, jump target, after a jump","Blocks to flow graph, loops are back edges","Block DAG removes CSE and dead code","Graph colouring allocates, spills on pressure","getReg with register and address descriptors"],"answer":"A basic block is a maximal straight-line sequence with one entry and one exit. Leaders are the first instruction, any jump target, and any instruction after a jump; a block runs from a leader up to the instruction before the next leader. For the given code, read x, i = 1, sum = 0 and prod = 1 form B1; the loop body from square = i*i to i = i+1 forms B2; write sum, write prod and halt form B3. The flow graph has B1 to B2, B2 back to itself as a back edge, B2 to B3 on exit, and B1 to B3 on the skip branch. A DAG per block removes common subexpressions and dead assignments. Code generation maps the IR under target constraints: instruction set, registers and addressing modes; registers are allocated by graph colouring with spilling, and peephole optimisation cleans the code."},{"n":9,"name":"Code Optimization","hook":"Correct code is not good code; optimisation rewrites the IR for speed and size, but only where the rewrite is provably safe.","steps":[{"t":"Name the waste","d":"The IR from Unit 7 recomputes values, keeps dead assignments and repeats loop work, so time and memory are spent for nothing."},{"t":"Set the criteria first","d":"An optimisation must preserve observable behaviour, must repay its own cost, and must not change when errors are reported."},{"t":"Clean up inside a block","d":"Constant folding and propagation, copy propagation, common subexpression elimination and dead code removal give cheap local wins."},{"t":"Attack the loops","d":"Loops dominate run time, so loop-invariant code motion, strength reduction, induction variable elimination and unrolling pay the most."},{"t":"Go global with data flow","d":"Reaching definitions, live variables, available expressions and def-use chains extend the same rewrites across blocks and procedures."},{"t":"Finish on the target code","d":"Register allocation and peephole rewriting are machine dependent, which is why the paper separates machine-independent from machine-dependent techniques."}],"keys":[{"k":"Code optimisation","v":"Transformation of the IR that improves time or space without changing observable behaviour."},{"k":"Criteria","v":"Preserve semantics, be worth its cost, and not delay or hide error detection."},{"k":"Constant folding","v":"Evaluate constant expressions at compile time, so 2*3.14 becomes 6.28."},{"k":"Constant propagation","v":"Replace a variable known to hold a constant by that constant, enabling further folding."},{"k":"Common subexpression elimination","v":"Compute a repeated expression once and reuse the earlier result."},{"k":"Dead code elimination","v":"Delete assignments whose results are never used afterwards."},{"k":"Copy propagation","v":"After x = y, replace later uses of x by y, often making the copy dead."},{"k":"Strength reduction","v":"Replace an expensive operation by a cheap one: x*2 becomes x<<1, x**2 becomes x*x."},{"k":"Loop-invariant code motion","v":"Hoist statements whose operands do not change inside the loop to just before it."},{"k":"Peephole optimisation","v":"Rewrite a small window of target code: redundant load/store, jump to jump, unreachable code."}],"traps":["Calling a rewrite safe without checking it preserves observable behaviour.","Removing a dead assignment whose right side calls a function with side effects.","Hoisting an expression that is not defined on every path through the loop.","Confusing strength reduction (x*2 to x<<1) with algebraic simplification (x*1 to x)."],"asks":["Why is code optimization needed? Describe any two loop optimization techniques.","What are the advantages of code optimization? Define dead-code elimination.","Explain the following code optimization techniques with examples.","What is peephole optimization? Explain redundant load/store, constant folding and strength reduction.","Explain the need and criteria for code optimization.","What are the techniques for compiler optimization? Explain."],"skel":["Need: speed, size, memory, cache","Criteria: semantics, profitability, no delay of errors","Local: folding, propagation, CSE, dead code","Loops: invariance, strength reduction, induction","Global: reaching defs, live variables, available exprs","Machine dependent: registers, peephole"],"answer":"Optimisation rewrites the intermediate representation to make the target program faster or smaller, subject to three criteria: it must preserve observable behaviour, it must be worth its cost, and it must not change when errors are detected. Local techniques work inside a basic block. Constant folding evaluates 2*3.14 to 6.28 and constant propagation replaces a variable known to hold a constant. Copy propagation turns b = a; c = b into c = a, after which b = a is dead and is removed by dead code elimination. Common subexpression elimination computes a*b once when it repeats. Strength reduction rewrites x*2 as x<<1 and x**2 as x*x. Loops matter most: loop-invariant code motion hoists statements whose operands do not change, induction variable elimination replaces i*4 by an additive counter, and unrolling cuts branch overhead. Global versions require data flow analysis, and peephole optimisation finishes on the target code."},{"n":10,"name":"Case Studies: C and C++ Compilers","hook":"Real compilers are the same six phases regrouped into three reusable stages, and everything that makes C++ painful lives in the front end.","steps":[{"t":"Six phases become three stages","d":"A production compiler groups the phases as front end, middle end and back end, so several languages can share one optimiser and one code generator."},{"t":"Front end lowers to a common form","d":"GCC parses C into GENERIC trees, then simplifies them into GIMPLE, a form suited to analysis and to static single assignment."},{"t":"Middle end optimises once for all languages","d":"Passes over GIMPLE do constant propagation, dead code elimination, global value numbering, inlining and loop optimisation, with no knowledge of C or C++."},{"t":"Back end is retargeted, not rewritten","d":"GIMPLE expands to RTL, registers are allocated, instructions scheduled, and machine descriptions let one compiler emit for many CPUs."},{"t":"C++ pays in the front end","d":"Name mangling, template instantiation, overload resolution, vtables, exception tables and RAII all happen before lowering, so C++ compiles slower."},{"t":"LLVM and JIT repeat the split","d":"A language-independent SSA IR plus a pass manager serves Clang, Rust and Swift, and hot paths can be compiled at run time instead of ahead of time."}],"keys":[{"k":"GENERIC and GIMPLE","v":"GCC's language-independent tree form and its simplified, SSA-friendly form used by the middle end."},{"k":"Middle end","v":"Machine-independent optimisation passes over GIMPLE, shared by every language GCC supports."},{"k":"RTL","v":"GCC's low-level register-transfer representation, from which the back end emits assembly."},{"k":"Retargetability","v":"Machine descriptions supply instruction patterns, so a new CPU needs a back end, not a compiler."},{"k":"Name mangling","v":"Encoding parameter types in a symbol name so overloaded functions get distinct assembler labels."},{"k":"Template instantiation","v":"Compiling a separate copy of a template for each type used; a compile-time and code-size cost."},{"k":"vtable","v":"Per-class table of virtual function pointers that makes dynamic dispatch possible."},{"k":"Unwinding tables","v":"Metadata that lets the run time unwind frames for exceptions without cost on the normal path."},{"k":"LLVM IR","v":"Language-independent SSA intermediate representation with a modular pass manager and ORC JIT."},{"k":"JIT compilation","v":"Compiling hot paths at run time using profile data, with deoptimisation when assumptions fail."}],"traps":["Describing a C++ compile as a different pipeline; the middle and back ends are shared with C.","Saying templates cost run time; the cost is compile time and object size.","Calling exception handling free; it is zero-cost on the normal path but not in table size.","Naming LLVM a language rather than an IR and compiler infrastructure."],"asks":["Compare the structure of C and C++ compilers with a block diagram.","Discuss the front end, middle end and back end of GCC.","Why does a C++ program compile more slowly than the equivalent C program?","Write short notes on LLVM and JIT compilation.","Explain the case study of a C compiler with its optimisation passes.","What is name mangling and why is it needed?"],"skel":["Three stages: front, middle, back","GCC: GENERIC to GIMPLE, SSA form","Middle end passes are language independent","Back end: RTL, registers, machine descriptions","C++ front end: mangling, templates, vtables, EH","LLVM: one SSA IR, pass manager, JIT"],"answer":"C and C++ compilers share one three-stage structure; almost all the difference sits in the front end. GCC's C front end parses into GENERIC and lowers it to GIMPLE, a simplified form supporting static single assignment. The middle end runs machine-independent passes over GIMPLE: constant propagation, dead code elimination, global value numbering, inlining and loop optimisation. The back end expands GIMPLE to RTL, allocates registers, schedules instructions and emits assembly, using machine descriptions, which is what makes the compiler retargetable to a new CPU. A C++ front end must additionally mangle names so overloads get distinct symbols, instantiate templates once per type used, resolve overloads, build vtables for virtual dispatch, emit exception unwinding tables for RAII, and manage constructors and destructors. That front-end work is why C++ compiles more slowly and ships larger objects. LLVM repeats the same split with one SSA IR, a modular pass manager and a JIT."}],"egov":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to E-Government and E-Governance","hook":"The first job is to stop you using e-government and e-governance as synonyms, because every later unit inherits that distinction.","steps":[{"t":"Computerise the record","d":"A department computerises its registers first, because a service cannot be online while the record behind it is paper."},{"t":"See the office as a system","d":"The computerised office is an information system: citizen data in, rules and workflows through, certificates and payments out, complaints back as feedback."},{"t":"Split the scope","d":"Once output leaves the counter, the scope splits: e-government is that service; e-governance is the whole governing process it sits inside."},{"t":"Repeat as a life cycle","d":"The life cycle then repeats — initiation, planning, implementation, operation, monitoring, improvement — so each service becomes the input to the next."},{"t":"Test against benefits and barriers","d":"Benefits are claimed per actor, and barriers such as the digital divide decide how far the cycle can run — which is exactly what the paper asks."}],"keys":[{"k":"E-Government","v":"ICT, mainly the internet, used by government to deliver services and information to citizens, business and other agencies."},{"k":"E-Governance","v":"ICT applied to the whole governing process — service delivery plus participation, transparency, accountability and responsiveness."},{"k":"G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E","v":"Government to citizens, to business, to other agencies, and to its own employees."},{"k":"Information-system view","v":"Inputs of citizen data, processing by rules and workflows, service outputs, and feedback that reshapes the process."},{"k":"Electronic vs online delivery","v":"Electronic covers every channel — kiosk, SMS, IVR, ATM, call centre; online is the internet subset of it."},{"k":"E-government life cycle","v":"Initiation, planning and design, implementation, operation, monitoring and evaluation, then improvement."},{"k":"Digital divide","v":"The gap between those with connectivity, devices and skills and those without, which excludes them from e-services."},{"k":"Evolution stages","v":"Record computerisation, networking, informational websites, interactive portals, transactional services, then integrated participatory governance."}],"traps":["Writing e-government and e-governance as synonyms, then losing the whole ten-mark differentiation question.","Calling online delivery the whole of electronic service delivery, when kiosks and SMS are also electronic.","Listing benefits as one block instead of splitting citizen, government and society, which the marking scheme expects.","Confusing the evolution stages with the life cycle; one is history, the other is a project."],"asks":["Differentiate between e-Government and e-Governance. What are the various stages of e-governance?","Define E-Government. Explain E-Government as an Information System.","What are the benefits of using E-Governance for both government and citizens?","What is the difference between online service delivery and electronic service delivery?","What are the present global trends of growth in E-Governance? Discuss with examples."],"skel":["Define e-government by ICT, internet, service delivery, four channels","Define e-governance as ICT applied to the whole governing process","Contrast scope, nature, focus, actors, example in a table","Draw input, process, output, feedback as an information system","Split benefits by citizen, government, society; then add barriers","Close with the life cycle, initiation through continuous improvement"],"answer":"E-government is the use of ICT by government to deliver services and information to citizens, businesses and other agencies — the G2C, G2B, G2G and G2E channels. E-governance is wider: ICT applied to the whole governing process, including participation, transparency and accountability. So an online passport form is e-government; a published budget opened for public comment is e-governance. Seen as an information system, e-government takes citizen data and applications as input, validates them through workflows and databases, produces certificates, payments and information as output, and returns complaints and audit findings as feedback that reshapes the process. Benefits split by actor: citizens get 24x7 single-window access and less travel; government gets lower cost, faster coordination and auditable decisions; society gets inclusion and trust. The barriers — digital divide, privacy, connectivity, staff resistance — are why maturity matters."},{"n":2,"name":"Models of E-Governance","hook":"Five models and five maturity levels look like ten things to memorise but are one idea — how far information travels and how deep the transaction goes.","steps":[{"t":"Start with one-way push","d":"Level zero of participation is broadcasting: government pushes information out to everyone, one-to-many, with no way for the citizen to answer."},{"t":"Aim the push at watchdogs","d":"Critical flow keeps that one-way arrow but changes the audience — journalists, auditors, courts — so disclosed data can actually punish wrongdoing."},{"t":"Turn data into a mirror","d":"Comparative analysis reuses the same published data as comparison: district rankings and report cards create pressure without any citizen transaction."},{"t":"Reverse the arrow","d":"Mobilisation and lobbying turn the arrow around: e-petitions and consultations carry citizen demand upward and set the agenda."},{"t":"Close the circuit","d":"Interactive service closes it — the citizen transacts, the transaction updates the back office, a response returns; G2C2G is the highest of the five."},{"t":"Read maturity as the same arrow","d":"The levels trace the identical arrow: publish, enrich, interact, transact, integrate — same progression, measured in depth rather than direction."}],"keys":[{"k":"Broadcasting model","v":"One-to-many push of information to a wide audience through portals, SMS and media; no feedback."},{"k":"Critical flow model","v":"High-value sensitive information routed to those who can act on it — media, auditors, courts, watchdogs."},{"k":"Comparative analysis model","v":"Government data compared across districts, departments or years to create pressure for improvement."},{"k":"Mobilisation and lobbying","v":"ICT used to organise citizens, build coalitions and push demands into the policy agenda."},{"k":"Interactive service (G2C2G)","v":"Two-way transaction where citizen action updates the back office and a response returns; highest of the five."},{"k":"Level 1, emerging","v":"Static websites publishing brochures and notices; one-way, no interaction, no searchable data."},{"k":"Level 2, enhanced","v":"More content, searchable databases and downloadable forms, but still a one-way information flow."},{"k":"Level 3, interactive","v":"Two-way communication begins — e-mail, online queries, forms — but payment and issuance stay offline."},{"k":"Level 4, transactional","v":"Full transaction online with secure authentication: payment, certificates and licences issued without a counter visit."},{"k":"Level 5, connected","v":"Departments integrated behind a single window, data entered once, services proactive and participatory."}],"traps":["Treating critical flow and broadcasting as the same; both are one-way, but the audience and purpose differ.","Calling level three transactional; payment and certificate issuance only begin at level four.","Naming maturity levels inside the models answer, or models inside the maturity answer.","Forgetting that G2C2G includes the return path — the transaction must change the back office."],"asks":["What is a digital governance model? Describe the Critical Flow Model. How does it differ from other models?","Explain the five models of e-governance with practical examples.","What is the E-Governance maturity model? Explain the levels with their characteristics.","How can we achieve good governance through e-governance models?","Describe the five maturity levels. At which level do you think Nepal currently stands?"],"skel":["Models describe the direction in which information flows","Broadcasting pushes out; critical flow aims the same push at watchdogs","Comparative analysis creates pressure; mobilisation reverses the arrow","Interactive service closes the loop; G2C2G is the highest","Levels measure depth: emerging, enhanced, interactive, transactional, connected","One closing paragraph linking each model to a good-governance value"],"answer":"Digital governance models describe the direction in which information flows; maturity levels describe how deep that flow goes. Broadcasting pushes information one way to many — a ministry site or SMS alert — and stops there. Critical flow keeps the one-way push but targets watchdogs: tenders, asset declarations, pollution data, so wrongdoing can be punished. Comparative analysis turns the same government data into district rankings or citizen report cards, creating pressure without any transaction. Mobilisation and lobbying reverse the arrow: e-petitions and consultations let citizens set the agenda. Interactive service (G2C2G) closes the loop — a citizen files and pays online, and that transaction changes the back office. The five maturity levels track the identical climb: emerging with static pages, enhanced with downloadable forms, interactive with queries, transactional with authenticated payment and certificates, connected with integration and no repeated data. Nepal sits around level three."},{"n":3,"name":"E-Government Infrastructure Development","hook":"A portal is the last five per cent of e-government; this unit is the ninety-five per cent of wiring, law and institutions underneath it.","steps":[{"t":"Connect first","d":"A service cannot exist without a network, so connectivity — fibre backbone, district links, internet gateways — is built before any portal."},{"t":"Put machines at both ends","d":"A network needs machines: servers, virtualisation and cloud behind, devices and kiosks in front, all of it computing infrastructure."},{"t":"Concentrate the machines","d":"Those machines gather in a data centre, because power, cooling, access control and disaster recovery are cheaper shared than per ministry."},{"t":"Impose an architecture","d":"Shared hosting forces a blueprint: access, service, application, integration, data and infrastructure layers, with security and standards cutting across."},{"t":"Make the layers talk","d":"Layers only talk if interoperability is mandated — shared identifiers, APIs and schemas — otherwise every ministry rebuilds the same silo."},{"t":"Add the non-technical layer","d":"Hardware decides nothing alone, so e-readiness adds law, institutions, skills and affordability; that gap is why the paper separates infrastructure from preparedness."}],"keys":[{"k":"Network infrastructure","v":"Fibre backbone, national broadband, LAN/WAN, intranet, internet gateways, redundancy and last-mile connectivity."},{"k":"Computing infrastructure","v":"Servers, virtualisation, cloud in IaaS, PaaS and SaaS forms, data centres, end-user devices and service desks."},{"k":"Data centre","v":"Centralised hosting with tiered power, cooling, fire suppression, access control, backup and a disaster-recovery site."},{"k":"Six architecture layers","v":"Access, service, application, integration, data and infrastructure, with security and standards cutting across all."},{"k":"Interoperability framework","v":"Standards, identifiers, APIs and a governing body that let separate systems exchange data without custom glue."},{"k":"Cloud governance","v":"Policy on adoption, data residency, vendor lock-in, service levels, security, cost control and exit strategy."},{"k":"E-readiness","v":"How far a country is prepared: infrastructure, affordability, skills, legal framework, institutions and government adoption."},{"k":"EGDI","v":"UN E-Government Development Index, scoring online service presence, telecom infrastructure and human capital."},{"k":"Data system infrastructure","v":"Citizen, land and business registries plus standards, data quality, warehouse, backup and open data."},{"k":"Legal preparedness","v":"Electronic transaction, digital signature, data protection, cybercrime and RTI laws that make e-records admissible."}],"traps":["Describing only hardware and calling it infrastructure; the paper expects legal, institutional and human preparedness too.","Confusing architecture layers with infrastructure components; one is a blueprint, the other is equipment.","Defining interoperability as a shared server rather than standards, identifiers and APIs.","Reducing e-readiness to internet penetration and ignoring affordability, skills and law."],"asks":["What different infrastructure are necessary to implement E-governance? Explain computing infrastructure.","What is an Interoperability Framework? Why is it important?","What is e-readiness? Discuss the data system infrastructural preparedness.","Explain cloud governance in the context of e-government infrastructure.","What is institutional infrastructural preparedness? Why is it important for e-governance?"],"skel":["Network, then computing, then data centre, then the architecture layers","Name the six layers and the cross-cutting security band","Interoperability: identifiers, APIs, schemas, payment gateway, governance body","Cloud governance: residency, service levels, lock-in, exit","E-readiness: infrastructure, affordability, skills, law, institutions","Anchor with Nepal's integrated data centre and the UN EGDI"],"answer":"E-government infrastructure is layered because each layer depends on the one below. Network — fibre backbone, district connectivity, intranet and internet gateways — carries the traffic. Computing — servers, virtualisation, cloud and end-user devices — runs the applications. Data centres host them with power, cooling, physical access control and a disaster-recovery site; Nepal's Government Integrated Data Centre is the local instance. Above that sits the architecture: access, service, application, integration, data and infrastructure layers, with security and standards cutting across all. Interoperability is what stops these becoming silos — shared identifiers, APIs, XML and JSON schemas, an e-payment gateway and a governing body. Cloud governance sets residency, service levels, lock-in and exit rules. Finally e-readiness judges whether the country can use all this: infrastructure, affordability, skills, legal framework and institutional coordination, scored by the UN EGDI."},{"n":4,"name":"Security for E-Government","hook":"The state holds everyone's data in one place, so a single weak layer is a national breach rather than a website defacement.","steps":[{"t":"Name the assets","d":"Start from assets: citizen registries, payment systems and service portals are what an attacker wants, so they define the risk surface."},{"t":"Make risk arithmetic","d":"Risk is threat times vulnerability times impact, which is why a legacy server with known holes outranks a hardened one regardless of value."},{"t":"Stack the controls","d":"Controls are stacked in depth so one failed layer cannot expose the data: physical, network, host, application, data and identity."},{"t":"Move the perimeter to identity","d":"Identity becomes the real perimeter once services are online — MFA, RBAC and digital signatures decide who reaches which record."},{"t":"Feed monitoring back","d":"Nothing stays secure unmonitored, so logs, SIEM and incident response feed an audit that reopens the risk assessment; that loop is PDCA."},{"t":"Anchor in standards and law","d":"ISO 27001 and national CERT rules make the loop auditable, and Nepal's Electronic Transactions Act 2063 makes a breach punishable."}],"keys":[{"k":"Defence in depth","v":"Several independent control layers, so one failure does not expose the data behind it."},{"k":"Security management model","v":"Governance, risk management, controls, incident response, audit, awareness and continuous PDCA improvement."},{"k":"Physical layer","v":"Data-centre access control, CCTV, guards and locked racks — the layer everyone forgets in the diagram."},{"k":"Network layer","v":"Firewall, IDS/IPS, VPN, segmentation and DDoS protection guard traffic between agencies."},{"k":"Application layer","v":"Secure coding, input validation, authentication and session management stop the portal being the way in."},{"k":"Data layer","v":"Encryption at rest and in transit, masking, retention rules and tested backups."},{"k":"Identity and access","v":"SSO, MFA, RBAC and PKI with digital signatures decide who reaches which record."},{"k":"ISO/IEC 27001","v":"Standard for an information security management system: risk assessment, controls, audit and continual improvement."},{"k":"Insider threat","v":"Authorised staff misusing legitimate access, which no firewall stops and only logging catches."},{"k":"National CERT","v":"Country-level team that watches threats, issues advisories and coordinates incident response."}],"traps":["Answering security with a firewall and antivirus; the marking scheme wants layers plus policy and people.","Listing challenges without the risk-based approach, or the approach without the architecture diagram.","Leaving out identity and access, which is where most real government breaches begin.","Forgetting that insider threat sits inside the perimeter and only logging and audit catch it."],"asks":["What are the challenges to E-Governance security? Explain.","Explain the E-Governance security model.","What is E-Governance security architecture? Explain with a diagram.","What are the different approaches to E-Governance security? Explain.","Discuss the significance of cyber laws in protecting government digital infrastructure and citizen data."],"skel":["List challenges: privacy, cybercrime, insiders, legacy systems, legal gaps","Approach: identify assets, assess risk, control, monitor, audit","Management model as a PDCA loop with awareness and training","Draw eight architecture layers, physical through to policy","Name ISO 27001, NIST CSF, OWASP and the national CERT","Close with ETA 2063 as Nepal's legal teeth"],"answer":"Security is hard for e-government because the state holds large, linked databases of citizens and must stay available while sharing data between agencies. The challenges are identity theft, cybercrime such as ransomware and DDoS, insider threats, legacy systems, legal gaps and a shortage of skilled staff. The approach is risk-based and layered. Identify assets, assess risk as threat times vulnerability times impact, apply controls, monitor, audit, improve. The management model runs governance and policy, risk management, control implementation, incident response, audit, awareness training and continuous PDCA improvement. The architecture stacks physical, network with firewalls and segmentation, host hardening, secure applications, data encryption, identity and access with MFA and PKI, monitoring and audit, and policy with people. Standards anchor it: ISO 27001 for the ISMS, NIST CSF, OWASP for applications. Nepal's Electronic Transactions Act 2063 supplies the legal teeth."},{"n":5,"name":"Data Warehousing and Data Mining in Government","hook":"Government already collects the data; the warehouse and the mine are how a ministry turns ten years of records into one decision.","steps":[{"t":"Notice the transactional limit","d":"Daily systems record transactions one at a time and keep only the present, so nobody can query ten years of anything."},{"t":"Lift the data out","d":"ETL copies that data out, cleans and conforms it, and loads it into a subject-oriented, time-variant, non-volatile store."},{"t":"Ask aggregate questions","d":"Because the store is historical, OLAP and data marts answer enrolment by district or inflation by month, which OLTP never could."},{"t":"Go beyond the query","d":"Mining then goes further: classification, clustering, association rules and outlier detection surface patterns nobody thought to ask for."},{"t":"Scale to a national warehouse","d":"National warehouses apply this at scale — census for delimitation and allocation, commodity prices for inflation and supply."},{"t":"End in a decision","d":"The output is a decision, not a report: subsidy targeting, outbreak prediction, budget allocation — hence the paper's sector questions."}],"keys":[{"k":"Data warehouse","v":"Subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile store built to support management decisions, not daily transactions."},{"k":"Four characteristics","v":"Subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant and non-volatile — the definition marks every answer."},{"k":"ETL","v":"Extract from source systems, transform to clean and conform, load into the warehouse."},{"k":"Data mart","v":"A smaller warehouse slice for one department or subject, fed from the central store."},{"k":"OLTP versus OLAP","v":"OLTP: many small current transactions, normalised. OLAP: few complex historical queries, denormalised star schema."},{"k":"Star schema","v":"Fact table of measures joined to denormalised dimension tables; a snowflake normalises the dimensions further."},{"k":"Data mining","v":"Extracting previously unknown, valid and potentially useful patterns from large data sets."},{"k":"CRISP-DM","v":"Business understanding, data understanding, preparation, modelling, evaluation, deployment — the mining process."},{"k":"Census data warehouse","v":"Population, household, literacy, housing and migration data used for delimitation, allocation and SDG monitoring."},{"k":"Commodity price warehouse","v":"Daily market prices collected from districts, used for inflation monitoring, supply decisions and consumer alerts."}],"traps":["Calling the operational database a data warehouse; it is current and volatile, the warehouse is historical.","Mixing OLTP and OLAP characteristics in one sentence, which loses the comparison marks.","Describing mining techniques with no government sector example attached.","Skipping ETL and jumping to dashboards, so the integration logic disappears."],"asks":["What do you mean by data warehousing and data mining? Explain applications in census and commodity prices.","Explain the concept of National Data Warehouses. How do they facilitate better governance?","How can we use data warehousing and data mining in agriculture and rural development?","Discuss how data warehousing and data mining is used in census data.","Explain the uses of data warehousing and data mining in health, education and rural development."],"skel":["Define the warehouse with Inmon's four characteristics","ETL from source systems into the store, then marts","OLTP versus OLAP, star versus snowflake schema","Mining techniques and the six CRISP-DM phases","Census warehouse: delimitation, allocation, SDG monitoring","Commodity prices: inflation, supply, consumer alerts"],"answer":"A data warehouse is a subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant and non-volatile store built for decision-making, not daily transactions. Operational systems — land records, tax, vital registration — hold current, normalised data. ETL extracts it, cleans and conforms it, and loads it into the warehouse, from which data marts and OLAP cubes serve reporting. Data mining then looks for patterns nobody asked for: classification, clustering, association rules, regression and outlier detection, following CRISP-DM. Government uses this in two flagship national warehouses. The census warehouse holds population, literacy, housing and migration data for delimitation, resource allocation and SDG monitoring. The commodity price warehouse collects daily market prices and drives inflation monitoring and supply decisions. Sector uses follow: crop-yield forecasting in agriculture, dropout analysis in education, outbreak prediction in health, leakage detection in rural schemes. The limits are data quality, silos and privacy."},{"n":6,"name":"Case Studies","hook":"Every case study is a maturity level with a budget and a failure mode, so answer them as evidence rather than as history.","steps":[{"t":"Ask what the case proves","d":"Every case study exists to answer one question: what does a maturity level look like in a real country, with real constraints?"},{"t":"Start where rent is extracted","d":"Land records come first because land is the highest-volume citizen record and the point where middlemen extract the most rent."},{"t":"Aggregate the counters","d":"Once one department is online the citizen still queues at ten counters, so single-window centres collapse them into one payment."},{"t":"Go where contact is frequent","d":"Local government follows because property tax, water bills and birth registration are the most frequent citizen-state contacts."},{"t":"Build the national layer","d":"Only then can a national layer connect the silos — national ID, one app, shared network and data centre underneath."},{"t":"Explain the stalls","d":"Cases that stall stall on law, connectivity or back office, so each success is really a story about an enabling condition."}],"keys":[{"k":"Bhoomi","v":"Karnataka land records: over 20 million RTCs online, printed at kiosks, removing the village middleman."},{"k":"e-Seva","v":"Andhra Pradesh one-stop urban centres for utility bills, certificates, reservations and licences."},{"k":"Smart Nagarpalika","v":"Municipal e-governance: property tax, water billing, birth and death registration, grievances and accounting."},{"k":"Nagarik App","v":"Nepal's single mobile front end pulling services from several ministries behind one login."},{"k":"NIC and NICNET","v":"India's National Informatics Centre runs the satellite network linking districts to the centre; backbone of Indian e-governance."},{"k":"CARD","v":"Andhra Pradesh computerised property registration with encumbrance certificates, valuation and slot booking."},{"k":"e-Khazana","v":"Treasury-bank integration for online budget release, payments, receipts and reconciliation in state finance."},{"k":"Hello Sarkar","v":"Nepal's grievance portal and hotline where citizens report service failures to the Prime Minister's Office."},{"k":"Electronic Transactions Act 2063","v":"Gives legal recognition to electronic records and digital signatures and defines computer crimes."},{"k":"Digital Nepal Framework 2076","v":"Nepal's eight-sector digital economy plan covering infrastructure, agriculture, health, tourism and governance."}],"traps":["Narrating a case's history without naming the maturity level it demonstrates.","Mixing Indian cases into a Nepal answer without saying they are models being learned from.","Describing a portal as success when the back office and legal mandate never changed.","Leaving out the Electronic Transactions Act 2063 in any Nepal e-governance answer."],"asks":["Write short notes on any four: Bhoomi, CARD, e-Seva, Smart Nagarpalika, e-Khazana, PRAJA.","Explain the e-governance initiatives in Nepal along with cyber laws.","Discuss the role of Smart Nagarpalika in promoting e-governance at the local level in Nepal.","What lessons can Nepal learn from the Bhoomi project in land records management?","Write a case study of e-governance in USA, China, Brazil or Sri Lanka."],"skel":["Open by tying each case to a maturity level","Bhoomi: land records, kiosk RTC, middleman removed","e-Seva and Ekal Seva Kendra: one counter, many departments","Smart Nagarpalika: property tax, billing, vital registration","Nagarik App and national ID as the integration layer","Close on ETA 2063, ICT Policy 2072, Digital Nepal Framework"],"answer":"Nepal's e-governance cases read best as maturity levels made concrete. Land revenue computerisation, learning from Karnataka's Bhoomi, puts the record of rights online so the citizen prints it from a kiosk and the middleman's discretion disappears. Single-window counters, following Andhra Pradesh's e-Seva, put many departments behind one counter for certificates, bills and licences. Smart Nagarpalika does the same at municipal level, computerising property tax, water billing and birth and death registration — where citizens meet the state most often. The integration layer is Nagarik App with the National ID, pulling services from several ministries behind one login, over NIC-style networks and the Government Integrated Data Centre. Hello Sarkar adds grievance redress. The binding constraint is legal: the Electronic Transactions Act 2063, ICT Policy 2072 and Digital Nepal Framework 2076 give electronic records and signatures validity, without which none of these transactions is enforceable."}],"ncc":[{"n":1,"name":"Language Preliminaries","hook":"C# looks like Java, but the marks sit in the type system: what lives on the heap, what dispatches at run time, and what the compiler merely rewrites.","steps":[{"t":"Source becomes an assembly","d":"Roslyn compiles each .cs file to MSIL plus metadata and packages it as a .dll or .exe; nothing has executed yet."},{"t":"CLR loads and verifies","d":"The runtime loads the assembly, checks type safety, lays out the heap and prepares the type's method table."},{"t":"JIT compiles on first call","d":"Each method's IL is translated to native code the first time it runs, then cached for the process."},{"t":"Memory splits in two","d":"Value types are copied inline; reference types are shared through a heap pointer that the GC tracks and reclaims."},{"t":"Dispatch decides behaviour","d":"virtual plus override reads the object's actual type at run time; new hides and binds by the reference type at compile time."},{"t":"Delegates carry the rest","d":"Delegates wrap methods, events wrap delegates, and LINQ, callbacks and async continuations are all built on them."}],"keys":[{"k":"MSIL (CIL)","v":"CPU-independent, stack-based instruction set plus metadata that the C# compiler emits into every assembly."},{"k":"CLI","v":"Specification behind .NET: CTS for types, CLS for cross-language rules, VES for loading and execution."},{"k":"CLR","v":"Runtime that loads assemblies, verifies type safety, JIT-compiles IL, manages memory and handles exceptions."},{"k":"JIT compilation","v":"Translates one method's IL to native code on its first call, then caches the result."},{"k":"Value vs reference type","v":"Struct, enum and primitives hold data inline; class, string, array and delegate hold a heap reference."},{"k":"override vs new","v":"override binds at run time through the method table; new hides the base member and binds by reference type."},{"k":"Struct vs class","v":"Struct is a value type with no inheritance and no null; class is a heap reference type with both."},{"k":"Abstract class vs interface","v":"Abstract shares fields and code under single inheritance; interface is a contract implementable many times."},{"k":"Delegate and event","v":"Delegate is a type-safe method pointer; event restricts it to add and remove from outside."},{"k":"Deferred execution","v":"A LINQ query runs only when enumerated; ToList forces it once and avoids re-querying."}],"traps":["Saying a struct always lives on the stack; it also sits inline inside a heap object.","Using .Result or .Wait() in async code, which deadlocks the request thread.","Claiming a sealed class cannot inherit; it cannot be inherited from, but it can inherit.","Writing that a partial class is joined at run time; the compiler merges the parts.","Treating an indexer as a property; it is a parameterised member reached with obj[i]."],"asks":["Explain the compilation and execution of a .NET application: CLI, MSIL and CLR.","Define lambda expressions and distinguish expression lambda from statement lambda.","Differentiate abstract class, interface and sealed class, and say when each is used.","What are indexers? How do they differ from properties? Explain with an example.","Distinguish collections from generics; explain named and positional attribute parameters.","Explain method hiding versus method overriding with a program."],"skel":["Source to Roslyn to MSIL plus metadata in an assembly","CLI defines CTS, CLS and the execution engine","CLR loads, verifies, JIT-compiles method by method","GC reclaims heap; value types copied, references shared","The same DLL runs on any OS with a CLR"],"answer":"C# source files are compiled by Roslyn into an assembly: MSIL, also called CIL, plus metadata, packaged as a .dll or .exe. MSIL is CPU-independent and stack-based, so it cannot run directly on any processor. The CLI is the specification that makes this portable: the CTS defines the type system, the CLS the subset every language must expose, and the VES the loading and execution rules. At run time the CLR loads the assembly, verifies type safety and lays out the heap. The JIT compiler then translates each method's IL into native code the first time that method is called, and caches it. That is why one build runs on Windows, Linux or macOS. Garbage collection reclaims heap objects, while exceptions, security checks and cross-language inheritance come from the runtime rather than the source language. Compile once, JIT per machine is the sentence to remember."},{"n":2,"name":"Introduction to ASP.NET","hook":"Every which-framework, which-command question is really asking whether you know .NET Core is a runtime plus a CLI, not a Windows product.","steps":[{"t":"Framework was Windows-bound","d":"The original .NET Framework shipped with Windows, ended at 4.8 and depended on IIS for web work."},{"t":"Core rebuilds the runtime","d":"Microsoft rewrote it cross-platform and open source, split the BCL into NuGet packages and allowed side-by-side versions."},{"t":"Standard holds the contract","d":"A common API specification let one library target both runtimes instead of being forked."},{"t":"Web frameworks stack on top","d":"Web Forms handled events and ViewState, MVC separated concerns, Web API returned JSON over HTTP."},{"t":"Core unifies them","d":"ASP.NET Core puts MVC, Web API, Razor Pages, SignalR and gRPC on one middleware pipeline over Kestrel."},{"t":"The CLI runs the project","d":"dotnet new, restore, build, run, test, publish form the workflow the paper keeps asking you to list."}],"keys":[{"k":".NET Framework","v":"Windows-only runtime and class library up to 4.8, versioned with the OS and tied to IIS."},{"k":".NET Core / .NET 5+","v":"Cross-platform, open-source, NuGet-modular runtime with side-by-side versions and higher throughput."},{"k":"Mono","v":"Third-party CLI implementation for Linux, macOS and mobile, now largely merged into .NET."},{"k":".NET Standard","v":"API contract listing what every implementation must expose, so libraries stay portable."},{"k":"Web Forms","v":"Event-driven, ViewState-based legacy framework using drag-and-drop server controls, Windows and IIS only."},{"k":"ASP.NET MVC","v":"Pattern-based framework separating model, view and controller, with clean URLs and testable actions."},{"k":"ASP.NET Web API","v":"HTTP service framework returning JSON or XML; merged into MVC in ASP.NET Core."},{"k":"ASP.NET Core","v":"Unified cross-platform framework for MVC, Web API, Razor Pages, SignalR and gRPC on Kestrel."},{"k":"dotnet CLI","v":"new, restore, build, run, test, publish and add package, the whole workflow from one tool."},{"k":"Project layout","v":"csproj holds framework and packages, Program.cs hosts, then Controllers, Views, Models, wwwroot, appsettings."}],"traps":["Calling ASP.NET Core a version of ASP.NET; it is a different runtime and pipeline.","Saying Mono is Microsoft's cross-platform .NET Core; it is an older independent CLI port.","Confusing build with publish: build compiles locally, publish produces deployable output.","Treating Web API as a separate framework in Core; it is MVC with ControllerBase."],"asks":["Differentiate between .NET, .NET Core and Mono frameworks.","What are the logics behind the .NET architecture and design principles?","Explain the .NET CLI commands to build, run, test and deploy.","Differentiate .NET and ASP.NET frameworks. How do you test .NET Core applications?","Describe the ASP.NET Core architecture overview and how it differs from ASP.NET."],"skel":[".NET Framework: Windows, IIS, stops at 4.8",".NET Core: cross-platform, modular, side-by-side, open source","Mono: independent CLI port, now folded into .NET","Source to MSIL, CLR loads, JIT to native","CLI drives new, restore, build, run, publish"],"answer":".NET Framework is Microsoft's original Windows-only implementation, versioned with the operating system and capped at 4.8; it ships the class library and runs Web Forms and MVC through IIS. .NET Core is a rewrite: open source, cross-platform, modular through NuGet, side-by-side versions and much faster, continuing as .NET 5 and later. Mono began as a third-party reimplementation of the CLI for Linux, macOS and mobile, later used by Xamarin; much of it is now merged into .NET. .NET Standard was the API contract that let one library target both. Compilation is identical for all three: C# to MSIL plus metadata, the CLR loads the assembly, and the JIT compiles IL to native code per method. The CLI supplies CTS, CLS and the execution engine, which is exactly why a library built once can run on any of them."},{"n":3,"name":"HTTP and ASP.NET Core","hook":"ASP.NET Core is a state machine for one HTTP message, so tracing a single request through the pipeline answers half the paper.","steps":[{"t":"Browser writes the request","d":"It opens a TCP connection to port 443 and sends a request line, headers, a blank line and an optional body."},{"t":"Kestrel parses the message","d":"The server reads method, path, headers and body into HttpContext, before any framework code is involved."},{"t":"Middleware runs in order","d":"Exception handling, HTTPS redirect, static files, authentication and authorization each get a chance to short-circuit."},{"t":"Routing picks the endpoint","d":"The path is matched against the route table; constraints such as id:int reject nonsense and route values are filled."},{"t":"Controller binds and acts","d":"The action is invoked with parameters bound from route, query, form or body, then returns an action result."},{"t":"View becomes the response","d":"Razor renders the model into HTML inside the layout, and status, headers and body travel back over the same connection."}],"keys":[{"k":"HTTP","v":"Stateless request/response text protocol over TCP, normally port 80 or 443."},{"k":"Request line","v":"Method, space, request target, space, version: GET /Students/Edit/5 HTTP/1.1."},{"k":"Status codes","v":"200 OK, 201 Created, 301 or 302 redirect, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 500."},{"k":"Idempotent methods","v":"GET, PUT, DELETE and HEAD can repeat safely; POST cannot."},{"k":"Kestrel","v":"Default cross-platform asynchronous web server that turns raw bytes into HttpContext."},{"k":"Middleware pipeline","v":"Ordered components wrapped around the endpoint; any one may short-circuit the request."},{"k":"Routing","v":"Matches a URL to an endpoint, fills route values and enforces constraints such as id:int."},{"k":"Model","v":"Entities, view models and validation rules; it carries no presentation logic."},{"k":"View","v":"Razor .cshtml that renders the model as HTML inside a shared layout."},{"k":"Controller","v":"Selects and runs the action, orchestrates model and view, returns an action result."}],"traps":["Mixing up 401 and 403: unauthenticated versus authenticated but forbidden.","Registering UseAuthorization before UseAuthentication, so roles are never populated.","Saying MVC needs three projects or three tiers; it needs three responsibilities.","Forgetting that middleware order decides whether static files bypass authentication."],"asks":["Define HTTP. Explain the MVC pattern.","Explain the request and response message format with an example.","Describe the ASP.NET Core architecture overview and how it differs from ASP.NET.","How does ASP.NET Core MVC provide a patterns-based way to build dynamic websites?","Describe any four ways to read an HTTP response in ASP.NET Core."],"skel":["Request line, headers, blank line, optional body","Kestrel parses bytes into HttpContext","Middleware runs in order and may short-circuit","Routing selects endpoint and fills route values","Action returns result, view renders, response written"],"answer":"HTTP is a stateless request/response protocol, so every message must carry everything the server needs. A GET /Students/Edit/5 arrives at Kestrel, which parses the request line, headers and body into HttpContext. The middleware pipeline then runs in registration order: exception handler, HTTPS redirection, static files, routing, authentication, authorization. Each layer may short-circuit, which is why order decides behaviour. Routing matches the path to the Edit action of StudentsController and extracts id = 5. The controller is activated with its dependencies injected, the model binder fills the parameter from the route, and the action loads the student through a service. It returns View(model). Razor finds Views/Students/Edit.cshtml, renders it inside the layout, and the result writes status 200, content type and HTML back through the same pipeline. That separation of model, view and controller is what makes each part independently testable."},{"n":4,"name":"Creating ASP.NET Core MVC Applications","hook":"This is the paper's centre of gravity: controller, action result, routing, Razor, tag helpers, binding and DI are all one request seen from six angles.","steps":[{"t":"Scaffold fixes the conventions","d":"dotnet new mvc creates Controllers, Views, Models, wwwroot and Program.cs, so folder and naming decide behaviour before you write code."},{"t":"Routing matches the URL","d":"The pattern {controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?} turns /Home/Index into HomeController.Index with an empty id."},{"t":"Controller is activated","d":"The activator constructs the controller and injects scoped services such as the DbContext through its constructor."},{"t":"Binder fills the parameters","d":"Route values, query string, form fields and then JSON body are matched by name, with annotations checked into ModelState."},{"t":"Action returns a result","d":"The method runs business logic and returns View(model), Json(data), RedirectToAction or NotFound, never raw HTML."},{"t":"Razor renders and responds","d":"The view engine finds Views/Home/Index.cshtml, Razor merges the model into _Layout, and the response is written."}],"keys":[{"k":"Controller","v":"Class named XController inheriting Controller, whose public methods become actions."},{"k":"IActionResult","v":"Interface for any result; ActionResult of T adds a typed model or status code for APIs."},{"k":"Action results","v":"View, PartialView, Json, Content, File, Redirect, RedirectToAction, NotFound, StatusCode, BadRequest."},{"k":"Conventional routing","v":"Pattern {controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?} applied app-wide by MapControllerRoute."},{"k":"Attribute routing","v":"Route attribute on controller or action, with constraints such as id:int or slug:alpha."},{"k":"Model binding sources","v":"Route, query string, form fields, JSON body, headers and files, matched by parameter name."},{"k":"ModelState","v":"Holds binding errors and annotation failures; IsValid is the server-side gate."},{"k":"Tag helpers","v":"Server-side attributes such as asp-for, asp-action and asp-validation-for that generate correct HTML."},{"k":"Razor","v":"Model directive, Model.Name expression, if and foreach blocks, RenderBody and sections in .cshtml."},{"k":"DI lifetimes","v":"Singleton one per application, scoped one per request, transient new on every resolve."}],"traps":["Thinking ModelState.IsValid checks the database; it checks binding and annotations only.","Leaving HttpPost off a form action, so the POST returns 405.","Returning View() after a successful POST, so refresh re-submits the form.","Registering DbContext as singleton, which shares one tracked context across all requests.","Assuming asp-for is optional; without it no name and id are emitted, so binding fails."],"asks":["How do you create a controller? Illustrate with an example.","What are the different action result types in ASP.NET Core MVC?","Explain model binding and server-side validation with data annotations.","What are tag helpers and what does a tag helper provide?","Design an API controller returning JSON for Product with GET, POST, PUT and DELETE.","Describe the procedure of rendering HTML with Razor with a scenario."],"skel":["Conventions map the URL to controller and action","Binder fills parameters from route, query, form, body","Annotations populate ModelState before the action runs","Action returns View, Json or redirect result","Razor merges model into layout and writes response"],"answer":"Model binding is the step that turns raw HTTP data into .NET objects. A POST /Student/Create first passes routing, which selects the action and fills route values; then the controller is created with its constructor dependencies injected. The binder fills action parameters by source: route values, query string, form fields, and the body for FromBody JSON; names must match property names, and nested or collection types bind recursively. As each property is assigned, data annotations run, Required, StringLength, Range and EmailAddress, and failures go into ModelState instead of throwing. The action checks ModelState.IsValid; if false it returns View(model), and tag helpers asp-for and asp-validation-for redisplay the input with its message. If true it saves and redirects. Client-side validation only mirrors this; ModelState remains the real gate, so the same rules are never trusted twice."},{"n":5,"name":"Working with Database","hook":"Two routes to the same table: ADO.NET where you write the SQL, EF Core where LINQ writes it, and the paper asks for both.","steps":[{"t":"The app needs rows","d":"ADO.NET exposes the primitives in order: a connection to open, a command to carry SQL, a reader or adapter to receive rows."},{"t":"Connected reading is a stream","d":"ExecuteReader keeps the connection open and walks rows forward-only, one at a time, which is fast and memory-light."},{"t":"Disconnected reading is a copy","d":"A DataAdapter fills a DataSet, the connection closes, edits happen offline and are written back later."},{"t":"Concatenation invites injection","d":"String-built SQL lets input change the statement, so parameters are sent separately from the text."},{"t":"ORM removes the mapping","d":"EF Core maps classes to tables and LINQ to SQL, with a DbContext tracking every loaded entity."},{"t":"One SaveChanges commits","d":"Add, Update and Remove mark state; SaveChanges writes the whole unit of work, and migrations keep the schema aligned."}],"keys":[{"k":"SqlConnection","v":"Opens and closes the physical database session; it must be disposed."},{"k":"SqlCommand","v":"Carries SQL text or a stored procedure name, plus parameters and an optional transaction."},{"k":"ExecuteNonQuery","v":"Runs insert, update or delete and returns the number of rows affected."},{"k":"ExecuteScalar","v":"Returns the first column of the first row, used for counts and sums."},{"k":"SqlDataReader","v":"Connected, forward-only, read-only row stream; one open reader per connection."},{"k":"SqlDataAdapter and DataSet","v":"Disconnected pair: fills an in-memory table set, edits offline, then updates back."},{"k":"Parameterised query","v":"SQL text and values travel separately, so input can never alter the statement."},{"k":"ORM","v":"Maps classes to tables and LINQ to SQL, hiding commands and manual row mapping."},{"k":"DbContext and DbSet","v":"DbContext is a unit-of-work session; each DbSet represents one queryable table."},{"k":"Migrations","v":"migrations add generates a schema change; database update applies it to the database."}],"traps":["Calling SqlDataReader disconnected; the adapter and DataSet are the disconnected pair.","Using AsNoTracking on entities you then update, so SaveChanges ignores them.","Concatenating input into SQL and calling it safe because a regex ran first.","Forgetting using or Dispose, so the connection pool is exhausted under load."],"asks":["Why do we need ORM? List the components of ADO.NET.","Write a function to fetch books with price greater than 1000 using ADO.NET.","Explain the role of Connection, Command, Reader and Adapter classes.","Create a table BAG and update, delete and retrieve rows using EF Core.","Create a method to insert a record onto Employee using Entity Framework.","List the advantages of Entity Framework over ADO.NET."],"skel":["Connection, Command, Reader, Adapter are the ADO.NET primitives","Reader is connected; Adapter and DataSet are disconnected","Parameters stop injection, concatenation never does","DbContext tracks entities, SaveChanges writes once","Migrations add and database update keep schema current"],"answer":"ADO.NET is the low-level route: SqlConnection opens the session, SqlCommand carries the SQL and its parameters, and rows arrive through SqlDataReader, which is connected, forward-only and one row at a time, or through a SqlDataAdapter filling a DataSet, which is disconnected so rows can be edited offline and written back. Parameters rather than concatenation are what stop SQL injection. An ORM removes the hand-written mapping: EF Core turns classes into tables, one DbSet per entity, and a DbContext that tracks changes as a unit of work. The same fetch becomes a LINQ query that EF translates to SQL and materialises on ToList; Add, Update, Remove and SaveChanges replace command objects, and migrations keep the schema in step with the model. ADO.NET gives control and speed for reports; EF Core gives development speed, relationships and change tracking. That trade-off is why ORM exists."},{"n":6,"name":"State Management","hook":"HTTP forgets you after every response, so each technique here answers one question: where does the memory live, and who is allowed to see it?","steps":[{"t":"The protocol keeps no memory","d":"Each request is independent, so a login cannot survive even one redirect without something stored and re-sent."},{"t":"Server memory with a key","d":"Session stores a per-user dictionary on the server and hands the browser only an opaque session cookie."},{"t":"Server memory for one hop","d":"TempData carries a message across a redirect and deletes it on read, which is exactly one request too many for ViewData."},{"t":"Memory for this request only","d":"ViewData, ViewBag and HttpContext.Items live and die inside a single request, so they never travel."},{"t":"Memory shared by everyone","d":"A cache stores expensive results app-wide with absolute or sliding expiry, trading freshness for speed."},{"t":"Memory handed to the client","d":"Cookies, query strings, hidden fields and local storage keep it in the browser, where it is visible and editable."}],"keys":[{"k":"Stateless HTTP","v":"Each request is independent; the server keeps no memory of the previous one."},{"k":"Session","v":"Per-user server dictionary keyed by a session cookie and dropped after an idle timeout."},{"k":"TempData","v":"One-shot storage that survives a single redirect and is removed when read."},{"k":"Peek and Keep","v":"Peek reads TempData without consuming it; Keep marks it to survive one more request."},{"k":"ViewData and ViewBag","v":"Controller-to-view data for the current request; ViewBag is dynamic and unchecked."},{"k":"HttpContext.Items","v":"Per-request dictionary used to hand data from middleware to a controller."},{"k":"IMemoryCache","v":"App-wide in-process cache with absolute or sliding expiry and explicit removal."},{"k":"Cookie","v":"Roughly 4 KB key-value in the browser, sent with every request to that domain."},{"k":"Query string","v":"Name-value pairs after the question mark; visible, bookmarkable and length-limited."},{"k":"Hidden field","v":"Input of type hidden posted back with the form; visible in source and editable."}],"traps":["Saying TempData lasts until the session ends; it dies one request after being read.","Calling ViewBag type-safe; it is dynamic, so a typo fails only at run time.","Putting a password in a query string or hidden field, both fully visible.","Assuming cookies hold megabytes; the practical limit is about 4 KB."],"asks":["Explain the parts of state management on the client side.","What is TempData? How is it different from Session and ViewData?","Explain the use of cache as a server-side state management strategy.","Write a program with pages P1 and P2 using query string and hidden field.","Give an example to manage the session state."],"skel":["HTTP is stateless, so state needs a store","Session: server memory keyed by a cookie","TempData: one redirect, then gone","ViewData and ViewBag: one request only","Cookie, query string, hidden field: client-held"],"answer":"Because HTTP is stateless, state must be stored somewhere and re-associated with the next request. Server-side, Session keeps a per-user dictionary on the server, keyed by a session cookie and expiring on timeout; it suits carts and logins but needs AddSession, UseSession and a distributed store on a farm. TempData carries a one-shot message across a redirect and is removed on read unless Peek or Keep is used. ViewData and ViewBag pass data only from controller to view within one request, ViewBag being dynamic and therefore unchecked. HttpContext.Items is a single-request hand-off between middleware and controller. IMemoryCache holds app-wide data with absolute or sliding expiry. Client-side, cookies are 4 KB key-values sent every request and must be HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite; query strings are bookmarkable but visible; hidden fields survive a postback but are editable; local storage holds 5 MB and is readable by any script."},{"n":7,"name":"Client-side Development","hook":"The browser is a second runtime, and how much validation, rendering and routing you push into it decides how much work never reaches the controller.","steps":[{"t":"Round trips are expensive","d":"Rejecting a bad form only after a full post wastes a request, so the first check moves into the browser."},{"t":"Rules are generated once","d":"Data annotations become data-val attributes in the HTML, and jquery.validate.unobtrusive reads them without new code."},{"t":"AJAX breaks the page cycle","d":"A jQuery call fetches JSON from an action or API and patches one region of the DOM instead of reloading."},{"t":"The server becomes an API","d":"Once JSON is the contract, routing, rendering and state can move into the client, which is the SPA idea."},{"t":"Frameworks differ in control","d":"Angular is an opinionated TypeScript framework, React a view library with one-way flow, Vue the gentle middle."},{"t":"Security stays on the server","d":"Client rules are convenience only; the same checks must run again because a request can bypass the browser."}],"keys":[{"k":"Unobtrusive validation","v":"jQuery validate reads data-val attributes emitted from data annotations, with no inline script."},{"k":"jQuery AJAX","v":"Sends a request asynchronously and chains done and fail handlers without a page reload."},{"k":"_ValidationScriptsPartial","v":"Shared partial that loads jquery.validate and the unobtrusive adapter on form pages."},{"k":"SPA","v":"One HTML shell; routing, rendering and state live in the browser, data comes from APIs."},{"k":"Angular","v":"Opinionated TypeScript framework with components, services, RxJS, DI and two-way binding."},{"k":"React","v":"Component library with hooks, one-way data flow and a virtual DOM diff for updates."},{"k":"Vue","v":"Progressive framework with template syntax and reactive binding, the easiest of the three to adopt."},{"k":"TypeScript","v":"Typed superset of JavaScript compiled to plain JavaScript, safer for large applications."},{"k":"Bundler","v":"webpack or Vite combines modules into few files and hashes them for caching."},{"k":"Server-side validation","v":"Repeats every rule on the server, because the browser can be bypassed entirely."}],"traps":["Claiming client validation is enough; the request can be forged without a browser.","Calling React a framework; it is a view library needing routing and state add-ons.","Thinking SPA means no server; it still needs APIs, authentication and static hosting.","Assuming ASP.NET Core requires jQuery; only the scaffolded validation scripts use it."],"asks":["How do you use jQuery for client-side form validation? Explain.","Validate username, password and a ten-digit phone number using jQuery.","What is SPA? Compare Angular and React with an ASP.NET Core backend.","Why do we need to validate the form? How can you use React for this?","Explain client-side scripting with a practical example."],"skel":["Client validation saves round trips, not security","Data annotations become data-val attributes","jQuery validate reads them, no duplicate rules","AJAX fetches JSON and patches the DOM","SPA moves routing and rendering to the browser"],"answer":"Client-side validation exists to save a round trip, not to secure anything. With jQuery, the form's submit handler reads each field, tests it and calls preventDefault on failure. A typical rule set: username and password non-empty, phone matching a ten-digit pattern, email matching a format, with an error span beside each input. ASP.NET Core automates this: data annotations on the model are emitted as data-val attributes by the tag helpers, and jquery.validate.unobtrusive reads them, so no rule is written twice; the shared validation partial loads both scripts. For data rather than messages, an AJAX call fetches JSON from a Web API controller and patches the DOM, which is the same idea SPAs scale up: Angular as an opinionated framework with two-way binding, React as a library with one-way flow and a virtual DOM, Vue in between. Server-side validation always repeats the checks, because any client can be bypassed."},{"n":8,"name":"Securing ASP.NET Core","hook":"Every attack in this unit is one mistake in different places: trusting something the client sent.","steps":[{"t":"Assume the request is hostile","d":"Anything can arrive: a forged form post, a script tag in a comment, or a quote that rewrites your SQL."},{"t":"Identity answers who","d":"Credentials plus optional second factor produce a cookie or token; passwords are stored only as salted PBKDF2 hashes."},{"t":"Authorization answers what","d":"Authorize attributes, roles, claims and policies are evaluated per request before the action is allowed to run."},{"t":"Encoding defuses XSS","d":"Razor encodes output by default, so injected markup is displayed as text instead of being parsed as code."},{"t":"Separation defuses injection","d":"Parameters keep data out of the SQL text; an anti-forgery token keeps foreign forms out of your session."},{"t":"Redirects need a local check","d":"returnUrl is validated with Url.IsLocalUrl, which closes the open redirect that phishing relies on."}],"keys":[{"k":"Authentication","v":"Establishes who the caller is, through a cookie or token after credentials are verified."},{"k":"Authorization","v":"Decides what an identified caller may do, per action, endpoint or resource."},{"k":"ASP.NET Core Identity","v":"Membership system with users, hashed passwords, roles, claims, two-factor, lockout and email confirmation."},{"k":"Password hashing","v":"PBKDF2 with a per-user salt and many iterations, so a stored hash cannot be reversed."},{"k":"Claims","v":"Name-value statements about a user, such as role, email or department."},{"k":"Policies","v":"Named rule combining several claims or requirements, applied with Authorize Policy."},{"k":"Anti-forgery token","v":"Hidden token plus matching cookie proving the POST came from your own form."},{"k":"XSS","v":"Injected script running in the victim's browser; stopped by encoding output, not input."},{"k":"SQL injection","v":"Input altering a concatenated query; stopped by parameters and least-privilege accounts."},{"k":"Open redirect","v":"returnUrl pointing to another site; blocked by an IsLocalUrl check before redirecting."}],"traps":["Saying input validation stops XSS; the actual fix is encoding on output.","Believing HTTPS prevents XSS or SQL injection; it only protects the wire.","Authorizing the controller but leaving a public GET that leaks the same data.","Treating HttpOnly as XSS prevention; it only hides the cookie from stolen script."],"asks":["Explain how ASP.NET Core defends against XSS through output encoding.","Explain the chances of a SQL injection attack with a scenario, and its prevention.","Define open redirect attacks and how to prevent them.","What is ASP.NET Core Identity? Explain adding authentication and configuring it.","Distinguish between roles and policies; define claim and policy.","Write code to define an admin role that can create, update and delete articles."],"skel":["Identity answers who, authorization answers what","Razor encodes output by default; avoid Html.Raw","Parameters keep data out of the SQL text","Anti-forgery token and SameSite stop CSRF","IsLocalUrl blocks open redirects"],"answer":"Output encoding defeats XSS by making injected text into data instead of markup. Razor's expression syntax HTML-encodes by default: a less-than sign becomes &lt;, so a comment containing a script tag renders visibly and never executes. Html.Raw bypasses that protection and must only receive trusted content; a Content Security Policy, HttpOnly cookies and input validation form the second layer. SQL injection happens when input is concatenated into a query, so a quote plus OR 1=1 changes the statement; parameterised commands and ORM queries send data separately from code, and a least-privilege account limits the damage. CSRF abuses the browser sending cookies automatically: another site posts to your form, so an anti-forgery token validated by ValidateAntiForgeryToken, together with a SameSite cookie, proves the request came from your page. Open redirect is the same trust error in navigation, fixed by checking Url.IsLocalUrl before redirecting."},{"n":9,"name":"Hosting and Deployment","hook":"The same DLL runs behind IIS, Nginx or a container, so the marks are in which process owns the socket and where the configuration lives.","steps":[{"t":"Publish produces the output","d":"dotnet publish writes the deployable folder, framework-dependent if the runtime exists on the host, self-contained if it does not."},{"t":"Kestrel owns the socket","d":"Whatever the front end, the application itself is served by Kestrel, the cross-platform asynchronous HTTP server."},{"t":"IIS picks a model","d":"In-process runs the app inside the worker process through ANCM; out-of-process reverse-proxies to a separate Kestrel."},{"t":"Linux puts a proxy in front","d":"Nginx or Apache terminate TLS, compress and load-balance, then forward plain HTTP to Kestrel on localhost."},{"t":"Docker fixes the environment","d":"A multi-stage Dockerfile builds in the SDK image, then copies only published output into a small runtime image."},{"t":"Azure runs the same artefact","d":"App Service takes that publish output, with slots, Key Vault and Application Insights for release and monitoring."}],"keys":[{"k":"Kestrel","v":"Cross-platform asynchronous HTTP server that hosts every ASP.NET Core application."},{"k":"In-process hosting","v":"App runs inside the IIS worker process via ANCM; fastest, but recycles with IIS."},{"k":"Out-of-process hosting","v":"IIS reverse-proxies to a separate Kestrel process; survives crashes but adds a hop."},{"k":"ANCM","v":"ASP.NET Core Module, the IIS native module that starts the app and forwards requests."},{"k":"Nginx","v":"Linux reverse proxy handling TLS termination, compression and load balancing in front of Kestrel."},{"k":"Dockerfile","v":"Ordered instructions that build an image layer by layer from a base image."},{"k":"Multi-stage build","v":"SDK image compiles and publishes; a smaller runtime image carries only the output."},{"k":"Container vs VM","v":"A container shares the host kernel; a VM runs a full guest operating system."},{"k":"Azure App Service","v":"PaaS that runs the published app, with slots, scaling and Key Vault integration."},{"k":"dotnet publish","v":"Writes the deployable folder, framework-dependent or self-contained, ready for the host."}],"traps":["Saying Kestrel cannot face the internet; it can, though a proxy is recommended.","Calling a container a small virtual machine; there is no guest kernel.","Confusing dotnet build with dotnet publish; only the latter is deployable.","Baking connection strings into the image instead of environment variables or Key Vault."],"asks":["Explain the hosting models, IIS, Nginx, Kestrel and Docker containerisation.","What is the ASP.NET Core Module and how does it work with IIS?","Explain the core differences between a virtual machine and a Docker container.","Describe the procedure of deploying a .NET Core application.","Write short notes on publishing an ASP.NET Core application to Azure."],"skel":["publish gives framework-dependent or self-contained output","Kestrel is the server; proxies sit in front","IIS in-process uses ANCM inside the worker process","Nginx terminates TLS and load-balances on Linux","Multi-stage Docker image runs identically everywhere"],"answer":"dotnet publish produces the deployable output: framework-dependent needs the runtime installed on the host, self-contained carries it. The application itself is always hosted by Kestrel, a cross-platform asynchronous HTTP server. With IIS on Windows there are two models. In-process runs the app inside the IIS worker process through the ASP.NET Core Module, which is faster because requests do not cross a proxy hop. Out-of-process keeps Kestrel as a separate process and ANCM reverse-proxies to it, which survives application crashes better. On Linux, Nginx or Apache terminate TLS and load-balance in front of Kestrel. Docker packages app and runtime into an image built in stages: the SDK image publishes, the smaller ASP.NET runtime image runs, so the container behaves identically everywhere and Kubernetes can scale it. Azure App Service takes the same publish output, adding slots, Key Vault and Application Insights."}],"tw":[{"n":1,"name":"What Is Technical Writing","hook":"A workplace document is judged by whether a stranger can act on it, not by how it sounds.","steps":[{"t":"Start from the reader's task","d":"Name what the reader must do afterwards: install, decide, approve, repair. That task, not the topic, sets the shape."},{"t":"Cut what does not serve it","d":"Strip ornament, hedging and self-expression until only the information the task needs survives. Conciseness and objectivity fall out of this."},{"t":"Fix the facts","d":"Verify figures, units, names and version numbers against a source. A wrong number destroys trust faster than clumsy prose."},{"t":"Impose visible order","d":"Add headings, lists, numbered steps and labelled visuals so a scanning reader can jump straight to their part."},{"t":"Test on a real reader","d":"Watch someone from the target group use the draft. Where they stall, the writing failed, not the reader."},{"t":"Name what you just did","d":"Each decision above is a named characteristic. The syllabus list is the audit trail of this process, which is why the exam asks for it."}],"keys":[{"k":"Technical writing","v":"Writing that conveys specialised information to a defined audience for a practical purpose: inform, instruct, persuade, record."},{"k":"Clarity","v":"Simple words, short sentences, one idea per paragraph, no ambiguity about who does what."},{"k":"Conciseness","v":"No padding; every word earns its place and the shortest accurate version wins."},{"k":"Accuracy","v":"Facts, figures, units and version numbers checked against a source before release."},{"k":"Audience awareness","v":"Vocabulary, depth and examples matched to what the reader already knows."},{"k":"Objectivity","v":"Evidence and a measured tone instead of opinion, hype or emotional appeal."},{"k":"Action orientation","v":"The reader can do something specific after reading; this is the success test."},{"k":"Creative writing differs by","v":"Purpose is to entertain or express; audience is general; success is how the reader feels."},{"k":"Academic writing differs by","v":"Argues for specialists, uses IMRaD, and is judged by peer acceptance, not task completion."},{"k":"Journalistic writing differs by","v":"Mass audience, inverted pyramid, success measured in readership and attention."}],"traps":["Listing ten characteristics with no example and no link to the reader's task.","Calling any hard writing technical; difficulty is not the same as technicality.","Saying technical writing is boring or jargon-filled — the standard is the opposite.","Confusing objectivity with having no recommendation; you can still advise."],"asks":["Define technical writing and explain its characteristics.","How does technical writing differ from creative and academic writing?","Distinguish between technical writing, academic writing and creative writing.","What features help make communication 'technical'?","Describe technical writing and elaborate on its characteristics."],"skel":["Definition: specialised information, defined audience, practical purpose","Ten characteristics, each named with a one-line example","Comparison table: purpose, audience, tone, structure, success test","Creative entertains, academic argues, journalistic attracts","Workplace email, memo, report and ticket are all technical writing"],"answer":"Technical writing communicates specialised information to a defined audience for a practical purpose — to inform, instruct, persuade or record. Its characteristics follow from that purpose. Clarity keeps one idea per sentence; conciseness removes padding; accuracy survives only if figures and units are verified; audience awareness matches vocabulary to the reader's knowledge; organisation uses headings, lists and numbered steps; objectivity prefers evidence to opinion; consistency fixes terminology and format; visual literacy puts a labelled table or diagram where prose would be slower; accessibility makes the document usable by a non-expert; action orientation means the reader can complete the task afterwards. Creative writing aims to entertain and is judged by feeling. Academic writing argues for specialists and is judged by peer acceptance. Journalistic writing chases mass readership through the inverted pyramid. Only technical writing is judged by whether the reader can act."},{"n":2,"name":"Audience and Purpose","hook":"A document that suits everyone suits nobody, so the writer must pick a reader and a single job.","steps":[{"t":"Name the primary reader","d":"The person who must act on the document. Everyone else is secondary (approves) or tertiary (overhears)."},{"t":"Profile what they know","d":"Rate knowledge as expert, technician, layperson or mixed, then decide how much background to supply and which terms to define."},{"t":"Find the decision they face","d":"Write the question the reader is asking, such as 'is this safe to deploy?'. Depth and evidence follow that question."},{"t":"State one purpose","d":"Choose inform, instruct, persuade, recommend or record. Two primary purposes produce a document that does neither well."},{"t":"Draw the scope fence","d":"List what is in and explicitly what is out, so the reader does not expect coverage you never intended."},{"t":"Choose the medium last","d":"Medium follows access, urgency, permanence and complexity; a phone alert and a PDF serve the same reader differently."}],"keys":[{"k":"Primary audience","v":"Readers who act on the document; they decide its content, level and tone."},{"k":"Secondary audience","v":"Readers who advise, approve or implement after the primary reader; often managers and reviewers."},{"k":"Tertiary audience","v":"Incidental readers who pick the document up later, such as auditors or new staff."},{"k":"Knowledge level","v":"Expert, technician, layperson or mixed; it sets how much you define and explain."},{"k":"Attitude","v":"Supportive, neutral or hostile; hostile readers need evidence early and no visible lecturing."},{"k":"Reader persona","v":"A one-page fictional profile of a typical reader, used to test draft decisions."},{"k":"Purpose","v":"One primary aim: inform, instruct, persuade, recommend, request, record or document compliance."},{"k":"Scope","v":"What the document covers and explicitly excludes: period, depth, geography, systems."},{"k":"Medium choice factors","v":"Access, urgency, permanence, complexity, cost, interactivity and legal need."},{"k":"Usability testing","v":"Watching real readers use the draft; the cheapest proof that the audience analysis was right."}],"traps":["Treating 'students' or 'users' as one audience; there are always at least three.","Choosing the medium first because it is familiar, then bending the purpose to fit.","Writing scope as a vague promise instead of an explicit in-and-out list.","Assuming the reader shares your technical vocabulary because your team does."],"asks":["Explain the concept of audience analysis in technical writing.","Develop an audience profile for a user manual of a mobile banking application.","What factors must a writer consider while analysing the audience?","How does understanding your audience affect purpose, scope and medium?","Technical writing is written for both internal and external audiences. Justify."],"skel":["Three audiences: primary acts, secondary approves, tertiary overhears","Six factors: knowledge, role, needs, attitude, context, constraints","Techniques: persona, interview, survey, usability test, SME, old documents","One primary purpose; secondary purposes must not compete with it","Scope states what is out as clearly as what is in"],"answer":"Audience analysis is the discipline of defining who reads a document before deciding what goes into it. Readers split into primary — the customer who transfers money and must succeed unaided; secondary — the bank's support staff and compliance officer who answer for the app; and tertiary — auditors and journalists who may read it later. For a mobile banking user manual the primary reader is a smartphone-literate adult with no banking-law knowledge, reading on the phone, often mid-task and anxious. That fixes everything. Knowledge level is layperson, so 'beneficiary' is defined on first use. Attitude is cautious, so security steps come before convenience features. Context is a small screen, so steps are numbered and illustrated. Constraints are low bandwidth, so the manual is a 900 KB PDF plus in-app help. Purpose is to instruct, scope covers login, transfer, statement and card block, and medium is in-app help rather than print."},{"n":3,"name":"The Writing Process","hook":"The process exists to keep rewriting cheap: structure is settled before sentences are polished.","steps":[{"t":"Plan before sentences","d":"Analyse audience and purpose, research, outline, choose medium, set a schedule. Rework here costs minutes."},{"t":"Draft fast and badly","d":"Follow the outline, write without editing, leave [TK] placeholders. Stopping to polish breaks the run and hides structural faults."},{"t":"Revise the whole","d":"Test content, accuracy, organisation, completeness and tone. Ask whether it does the job at all."},{"t":"Copyedit the sentence","d":"Then fix grammar, punctuation, consistency, wordiness and style-guide breaches, one category per pass."},{"t":"Publish and archive","d":"Format, proof the final file, distribute, archive the approved version, collect reader feedback."},{"t":"Loop back when the test fails","d":"Feedback returns you to planning, not to more polish. That is why the process is recursive and the exam asks for the order."}],"keys":[{"k":"Planning","v":"Audience, purpose, research, outline, medium and schedule; the stage where changes are cheapest."},{"k":"Drafting","v":"Fast writing from the outline without editing; placeholders allowed, flow preserved."},{"k":"Revising","v":"Whole-document pass on content, accuracy, order, completeness and tone."},{"k":"Copyediting","v":"Sentence-level pass: grammar, punctuation, spelling, consistency, wordiness, style guide."},{"k":"Proofreading","v":"Final check of the formatted file for typos, broken links and layout faults."},{"k":"Publishing","v":"Format, distribute, archive the approved version, and gather feedback for the next cycle."},{"k":"Recursive process","v":"Stages repeat; revision can send you back to planning, not just to rewriting."},{"k":"Collaborative writing","v":"Roles, shared outline, section division, one style sheet, one editor for voice."},{"k":"Version control","v":"One canonical file with named revisions so parallel edits cannot silently overwrite each other."}],"traps":["Editing sentences while drafting, which kills pace and hides structural faults.","Calling the process linear in the answer after the notes call it recursive.","Skipping copyedit because the content is right; marks live in the last ten per cent.","Listing collaborative advantages without the coordination costs."],"asks":["Discuss the writing process for technical documents.","Describe the technical writing process with its stages.","What are the key steps in the technical writing process?","Explain the copyediting and publishing phase; distinguish it from proofreading and peer review.","How does collaborative writing enhance or complicate this process?"],"skel":["Five stages: plan, draft, revise, copyedit, publish","Planning: audience, purpose, research, outline, medium, schedule","Drafting is deliberately unpolished; placeholders are fine","Revising is the whole document, copyediting the sentence, proofing the file","Feedback returns you to planning, so the process is recursive"],"answer":"Technical writing runs five stages, and the order exists to keep expensive work late. Planning fixes audience, purpose, scope, medium, outline and schedule; for an API guide that means deciding the reader is an integrator and the medium is a versioned web page. Drafting follows the outline at speed and leaves [TK] markers rather than stopping to polish. Revising then judges the whole document: is the authentication section complete, is the error table accurate, does the tone suit a developer? Copyediting follows, one pass each for grammar, terminology consistency and wordiness. Publishing formats, proofs the built page, archives the approved version and collects feedback. That feedback often returns the writer to planning, which is why the process is recursive rather than linear. Collaboration layers roles, a shared outline, one style sheet and one final editor over the same five stages."},{"n":4,"name":"Brief Correspondence","hook":"The format is the message: a memo, an email and a letter signal different audiences before a word is read.","steps":[{"t":"Decide internal or external","d":"Internal goes to a colleague or staff body; external goes to a customer, supplier or authority. That fact picks memo or letter."},{"t":"Pick the lightest medium that carries the weight","d":"Text for a confirmation, email for routine business, memo for policy inside, letter for anything formal or legally load-bearing."},{"t":"Choose the organisational plan","d":"Good news and routine go direct; bad news goes indirect through buffer, reasons, decision and close; persuasion uses AIDA."},{"t":"Build the header the format requires","d":"TO, FROM, DATE, SUBJECT for a memo; letterhead, date, inside address, salutation, close and signature for a letter."},{"t":"Write the purpose in line one","d":"The reader must know by the first sentence why this arrived. Context, detail and evidence follow."},{"t":"Close with the action and the date","d":"One request, one owner, one deadline, then a signature block and named attachments."}],"keys":[{"k":"Memo","v":"Internal document with TO, FROM, DATE, SUBJECT; no salutation and no complimentary close."},{"k":"Business letter parts","v":"Letterhead, date, inside address, salutation, subject, body, close, signature, enclosures."},{"k":"Full block format","v":"Everything flush left; the safest format for email-era correspondence."},{"k":"Modified block","v":"Date and complimentary close indented; body paragraphs flush left."},{"k":"Direct plan","v":"Main point first, then detail; used for good news and routine requests."},{"k":"Indirect plan","v":"Buffer, reasons, decision, positive close; used when the news will disappoint."},{"k":"AIDA","v":"Attention, Interest, Desire, Action; the four-move persuasive sequence."},{"k":"Email subject line","v":"Specific, six to eight words, states the action or topic, never left blank."},{"k":"Netiquette","v":"Reply within 24 hours, quote selectively, CC to inform, BCC for privacy, no ALL CAPS."}],"traps":["Putting a salutation and 'Yours sincerely' inside a memo.","Delivering bad news in the first line, then apologising for three paragraphs.","Using BCC to hide a complaint from the person being discussed.","Writing a letter when a two-line email would arrive in time."],"asks":["Prepare a memo instructing staff to shift to a new digital attendance system.","Compare a memo, an email and a business letter.","Write an email to your immediate boss about arrangements for the IT fair.","Write a letter to the Chief District Officer requesting permission to collect donations.","Compose a persuasive message in the AIDA method."],"skel":["Internal memo, external letter, quick email","Memo header: TO, FROM, DATE, SUBJECT","Letter: nine parts in a fixed order","Direct for routine, indirect for bad news, AIDA for persuasion","Purpose sentence first; action, owner and deadline last"],"answer":"MEMORANDUM\nTO: All Staff, Shikhar IT Solutions\nFROM: A. Sharma, Administration\nDATE: 13 November 2026\nSUBJECT: Digital attendance from 1 December\n\nFrom 1 December 2026 the biometric register at reception is replaced by the e-Hajira mobile app. Every employee must mark attendance on their own phone before 9:30 a.m. The app records location within office Wi-Fi range, so the paper register will not be accepted as a fallback.\n\nWhat you must do: install e-Hajira from the Play Store, log in with your staff ID and the temporary password sent today, and change the password on first login. Test your check-in on 28 November. Staff without a smartphone should collect a tablet from IT by 25 November.\n\nLogin problems go to it.support@shikhar.com.np, extension 214. Please finish installation before 27 November so December payroll is not delayed.\n\nContact: A. Sharma, extension 201."},{"n":5,"name":"Document Design and Graphics","hook":"Design is not decoration; it decides whether a reader under pressure can find the one number they need.","steps":[{"t":"Decide what the page must make easy","d":"Scan for a number, follow a procedure, compare options. The task sets the grid before any font is chosen."},{"t":"Set the grid and white space","d":"Margins, columns and consistent gutters; white space groups related items and gives the eye somewhere to rest."},{"t":"Build the hierarchy","d":"One title, consistent heading levels, type sizes that step down clearly. The reader should never guess the level."},{"t":"Apply CRAP","d":"Proximity groups, alignment anchors, repetition unifies, contrast separates. Four checks catch most ugly pages."},{"t":"Choose the graphic the data needs","d":"Line for trend, bar for comparison, pie for parts of a whole, table for precision, flowchart for process."},{"t":"Make each graphic stand alone","d":"Title, labelled axes with units, legend, source and caption. A reader who skips the prose must still get it."}],"keys":[{"k":"CRAP principles","v":"Contrast, Repetition, Alignment, Proximity; four tests for page-level design."},{"k":"White space","v":"Deliberate empty area that groups, separates and rests the eye; not wasted paper."},{"k":"Serif vs sans","v":"Serif for print body text; sans-serif for screens, headings and labels."},{"k":"Left-ragged alignment","v":"Most readable for body text; justified text creates rivers and uneven spacing."},{"k":"Line graph","v":"Trend of continuous data over time; the correct choice for growth or decline."},{"k":"Bar chart","v":"Comparison across discrete categories; the scale must start at zero."},{"k":"Pie chart","v":"Parts of one whole, five slices at most, never for comparing across charts."},{"k":"Flowchart","v":"Process and decision logic; diamonds for decisions, arrows for sequence."},{"k":"Stand-alone graphic","v":"Title, axes with units, legend, source and caption so it works without the prose."},{"k":"Mechanism description","v":"Purpose, overall description, parts and functions, principle of operation, maintenance."}],"traps":["Truncating a bar-chart axis to exaggerate a difference; that is a design and an ethics fault.","Using a pie chart for eight categories, or 3-D charts that distort area.","Deciding fonts and colours before deciding what the reader must find.","Adding a graphic that repeats the paragraph instead of replacing it."],"asks":["What role do document design principles play in technical communication?","Describe the key principles of designing effective graphics.","What factors should you consider when choosing between tables, bar charts, line graphs and flowcharts?","Describe the significance of graphics in presenting numerical data.","Make a flowchart diagram of your dream application."],"skel":["Design serves the reader's task, not decoration","Grid, white space, heading hierarchy, typography","CRAP: contrast, repetition, alignment, proximity","Match graphic to data: line, bar, pie, table, flowchart","Every graphic stands alone: title, units, legend, source"],"answer":"Design exists so a reader can find and use information without reading everything. Start from the task: a maintenance engineer needs torque values at 2 a.m., while a manager needs the cost total in ten seconds. Fix a two-column grid with 20 mm margins, which gives the technician a wide column for steps and a narrow one for warnings. Set one title, two heading levels and a 10/14 serif body; use sans-serif headings on screen. Then audit with CRAP: proximity groups each step with its diagram, alignment holds captions to one left edge, repetition keeps the warning box identical, and contrast makes the caution red on white only. For the data, choose a line graph for the five-year failure trend, a bar chart starting at zero for the three-site comparison, and a flowchart for the lockout procedure. Each carries a numbered title, units and source."},{"n":6,"name":"Writing for the Web","hook":"The web reader arrives mid-task from a search result and will leave before your second paragraph.","steps":[{"t":"Assume the reader arrives mid-task","d":"They come from a search result with one question and no patience. Write for that question, not for the site's history."},{"t":"Front-load the answer","d":"Inverted pyramid: conclusion in the first two lines, then supporting detail, then background."},{"t":"Chunk by task","d":"One page, one job. Split anything that serves two audiences or two decisions, then cross-link."},{"t":"Make it scannable","d":"Meaningful headings, bullets, bold keywords, two-to-four-line paragraphs and descriptive links."},{"t":"Write the words","d":"Active voice, second person, plain language, front-loaded keywords in headings and link text."},{"t":"Test and instrument","d":"Check mobile width, load time, alt text and contrast, then read search terms and drop-off data."}],"keys":[{"k":"Inverted pyramid","v":"Conclusion first, then evidence, then background; the web reader leaves before the end."},{"k":"Chunking","v":"One idea per short section or page; long pages split by task, not by org chart."},{"k":"Scannability","v":"Headings, bullets, bold keywords and short paragraphs so the page reads in a glance."},{"k":"Descriptive link text","v":"'Download the 2082 syllabus (PDF, 240 KB)', never 'click here'."},{"k":"Second person","v":"Address the reader as 'you'; it shortens sentences and clarifies who acts."},{"k":"One page, one task","v":"A page serving two jobs serves neither; split it and cross-link."},{"k":"SEO basics","v":"Title tag, meta description, one H1, clean URL, alt text, meaningful headings."},{"k":"Information architecture","v":"Site structure: navigation, hierarchy, labels and breadcrumbs that match user tasks."},{"k":"404 page","v":"Apologises briefly, explains, and offers search plus the three most likely destinations."},{"k":"Mobile-first","v":"Design at 360 px first; no horizontal scroll, 44 px tap targets, 16 px minimum text."}],"traps":["Writing a homepage as company history instead of a value proposition and one action.","Using 'click here' links, which fail screen readers and search engines alike.","Copying a print brochure to the web with the same paragraph lengths.","Hiding the price or the answer three scrolls down to build interest."],"asks":["What should one keep in mind when writing for the web?","What issues should a web designer be concerned about when planning a web page?","What is information architecture? Describe considerations for homepage, product page and FAQ.","Define FAQ and draft five FAQs for your website.","Write a proposal on redesigning a company website for user experience and accessibility."],"skel":["Web readers scan; they arrive from search, mid-task","Inverted pyramid, chunking, scannability, front-loaded keywords","Descriptive links, active voice, second person","One page equals one task; mobile-first, accessible, fast","Special pages: home, product, FAQ, contact, 404"],"answer":"Admission to B.Sc. CSIT — Frequently Asked Questions\n\nHow do I apply?\nApply online at entrance.tu.edu.np between 15 August and 5 September 2026, upload your Grade 12 transcript and pay the Rs 1,500 fee. Late forms are not accepted.\n\nWhat is the eligibility?\nGrade 12 Science with Mathematics, a minimum 50 per cent aggregate, or an equivalent qualification recognised by TU.\n\nIs there an entrance exam?\nYes. A 100-mark, two-hour MCQ paper on mathematics, physics, chemistry and English decides the merit list.\n\nWhat if I miss the exam?\nYou cannot be admitted that year. Keep the fee receipt and apply in the next cycle.\n\nCan I study while working?\nYes. Classes run 6:30 to 10:30 a.m., and about 40 per cent of students hold jobs.\n\nEach question is a heading, the answer opens in the first line, and every link names its destination and file size."},{"n":7,"name":"Information Reports","hook":"An information report records what is true; the moment it advises, it has become a different document.","steps":[{"t":"Fix the boundary: information, not advice","d":"An information report records and explains. Add 'we should' and it becomes a recommendation report."},{"t":"Pick the sub-type by what the reader needs","d":"Summary for managers, abstract for researchers, mechanism or process for operators, periodic or progress for tracking, news release for the public."},{"t":"Compress for the front","d":"Write the summary or abstract last, from the finished body, one sentence per section, numbers copied exactly."},{"t":"Order by the reader's question","d":"Mechanism: purpose, overall view, parts, operation, maintenance. Progress: done, doing, planned, problems, revised schedule."},{"t":"Use the inverted pyramid for public text","d":"A news release leads with who, what, when, where and why; quotes and boilerplate follow."},{"t":"Check the facts survive compression","d":"Every number in the abstract must match the body. A summary that drifts is a correction waiting to happen."}],"keys":[{"k":"Information report","v":"Presents facts and findings without recommending action; the recommendation report advises."},{"k":"Summary","v":"Plain-language restatement of the whole document for general or managerial readers."},{"k":"Abstract","v":"Technical condensation for specialists deciding whether to read; always placed first."},{"k":"Informative abstract","v":"States what was done and what was found, including results and numbers."},{"k":"Descriptive abstract","v":"States only what the document covers, with no findings; shorter than informative."},{"k":"Mechanism description","v":"Purpose, overall description, parts with functions, principle of operation, maintenance."},{"k":"Process description","v":"How something is done, step by step in time order; mechanism is how it works."},{"k":"Periodic report","v":"Fixed-interval report: period covered, activities, results, problems, next period's plan."},{"k":"Progress report","v":"One project's status: completed, in progress with percentage, planned, risks, revised schedule."},{"k":"News release structure","v":"Headline, dateline, lead with five Ws, body with quotes, boilerplate, media contact."}],"traps":["Writing 'we recommend' inside an information report and losing the definitional mark.","Confusing an abstract with an introduction; the abstract carries the findings too.","Describing a mechanism in time order, which quietly turns it into a process description.","Putting the company history before the announcement in a news release."],"asks":["What is an abstract? Write an informative abstract for a report on cybersecurity awareness.","Differentiate between a summary and an abstract.","Write a progress report on how you are accomplishing your goals.","Write a news release announcing the launch of a cloud-based student portal.","Describe the difference between a technical description and a technical definition."],"skel":["Information report records; recommendation report advises","Summary for managers, abstract for specialists, abstract always first","Mechanism: purpose, parts, operation. Process: steps in time","Progress: done, doing, planned, problems, revised schedule","News release: headline, dateline, lead, quotes, boilerplate"],"answer":"Abstract\n\nThis report examines cybersecurity awareness among 412 undergraduate students of a Kathmandu campus, surveyed in September 2026 with a 34-item questionnaire and eight follow-up interviews. Awareness was measured across password practice, phishing recognition, device security and data backup. Results show high familiarity with the term 'cybersecurity' at 91 per cent, but weak practice: 68 per cent reuse one password across accounts, 54 per cent could not identify a phishing link in a simulated message, and only 23 per cent back up coursework. Awareness correlates strongly with having taken a computing course (r = 0.62) and weakly with year of study. Students named social media as their main source of security information. The report concludes that knowledge is present but not converted into habit. It documents the survey instrument, sampling method and full response tables in the appendices for replication."},{"n":8,"name":"Employment Communication","hook":"A résumé is an argument that you can do this job, wearing the clothes of a list.","steps":[{"t":"Read the advertisement as a specification","d":"Underline every required skill and tool. Those words become the vocabulary of both documents."},{"t":"Choose the format from your history","d":"Steady relevant career takes chronological; gaps or career change takes functional; most applicants take combination."},{"t":"Select evidence, not duties","d":"Three to five achievements with a verb, a task and a number. Drop anything that does not answer the advertisement."},{"t":"Order the sections for a six-second scan","d":"Contact, summary, education, experience, projects, skills, extras, with reverse chronology inside each section."},{"t":"Write the cover letter as an argument","d":"Post, two proofs you fit, why this organisation, interview request. Never repeat the résumé word for word."},{"t":"Cut to one page and check ATS","d":"No tables or text boxes, standard headings, PDF, proofread. A parser reads it before a human does."}],"keys":[{"k":"Résumé","v":"One to two page targeted summary of relevant experience for a specific job."},{"k":"CV","v":"Full academic record that grows with the career; used for research and academia."},{"k":"Chronological résumé","v":"Reverse-time work history; best when the career is steady and relevant."},{"k":"Functional résumé","v":"Achievements grouped by skill; suits gaps, career change and freshers."},{"k":"Combination résumé","v":"Skills summary plus chronological history; the most common format today."},{"k":"Achievement statement","v":"Action verb, task, quantified result, as in 'reduced page load 40 per cent'."},{"k":"Cover letter structure","v":"Post applied for, two proofs of fit, why this organisation, interview request."},{"k":"ATS","v":"Applicant tracking software; needs plain headings, no tables, no text boxes."},{"k":"Thank-you letter","v":"Sent within 24 hours of an interview; specific, brief, one page."},{"k":"Resignation letter","v":"Dated, states the notice period, polite, no grievance, keeps the record clean."}],"traps":["Listing duties such as 'responsible for testing' with no result or number.","Sending one generic résumé to twenty employers; tailoring beats volume.","Adding a photo, marital status or date of birth when the employer did not ask.","Repeating the résumé word for word inside the cover letter."],"asks":["What is a résumé? Explain its types with advantages.","Compare and contrast chronological and functional résumés.","Construct a résumé for the position of IT officer in a company in the US.","Create a cover letter to apply for your dream job.","Write a job application and attach your recent biodata."],"skel":["Résumé targets a job; CV records a career","Chronological, functional, combination: pick by history","Sections: contact, summary, education, experience, projects, skills","Achievement equals action verb plus task plus number","Cover letter argues fit; it never repeats the résumé"],"answer":"Dear Ms. Rana,\n\nI am applying for the post of Junior Technical Writer advertised on your careers page on 5 November 2026. I completed my B.Sc. CSIT at Tribhuvan University in 2026 with a 3.72 GPA, and I have written documentation that developers actually used.\n\nAs an intern at Fuse Machines I rewrote 22 API reference pages in Markdown and cut support tickets on those endpoints by 31 per cent in two months. For my final-year project I produced a 40-page user manual and a five-minute installation video for a campus attendance system now used by 1,200 students.\n\nYour public style guide is the clearest I have read, and I would like to write inside it rather than around it. I have attached my résumé and three writing samples.\n\nI would welcome an interview at your convenience.\n\nYours sincerely,\nArun Karki\n+977 98XXXXXXXX"},{"n":9,"name":"Presentations","hook":"A talk fails in the planning, not on the stage: one sentence and three points beat thirty slides.","steps":[{"t":"Write the one sentence they must remember","d":"If you cannot say it in a sentence, you have a topic, not a message."},{"t":"Cut to three to five points","d":"Each point becomes a section with one piece of evidence. Everything else belongs in the handout."},{"t":"Choose the pattern","d":"Chronological, spatial, causal, problem-solution, comparison or topical; the pattern is the shape of the argument."},{"t":"Build slides to the 6x6 rule","d":"Six lines, six words, one idea per slide, 24 point minimum, charts instead of tables."},{"t":"Rehearse out loud with a timer","d":"At least twice, once recorded. Rehearse the opening, the transitions and the likely questions."},{"t":"Deliver to the room, not the screen","d":"Eye contact, about 130 words a minute, pauses, and the deck backed up on cloud, USB and PDF."}],"keys":[{"k":"6x6 rule","v":"At most six lines per slide and six words per line; a guard against slide reading."},{"k":"Core message","v":"One sentence stating what the audience should do or believe afterwards."},{"k":"Opening moves","v":"Hook, purpose, roadmap; the first 30 seconds decide whether attention survives."},{"k":"Transition","v":"A spoken signpost linking sections, such as 'that explains the cost; now the risk'."},{"k":"AIDA in talks","v":"Attention, interest, desire, action; the persuasive arc for a pitch."},{"k":"Speaking pace","v":"About 130 words a minute, with a pause after each main point."},{"k":"Slide legibility","v":"24 point minimum, high contrast, two fonts at most, no paragraph text."},{"k":"Backup plan","v":"Deck as PDF plus USB plus cloud; arrive early and test projector and clicker."},{"k":"Q&A handling","v":"Listen, repeat the question, answer briefly, park unknowns and follow up."},{"k":"Group presentation","v":"Sections by strength, one template, rehearsed handovers, one timekeeper."}],"traps":["Reading slides aloud, which doubles the load and empties the room.","Putting the whole report on slides because no handout was planned.","Rehearsing silently at the desk and meeting the time limit for the first time live.","Answering a hostile question with a long defence instead of the fact."],"asks":["Explain how you would plan and deliver an effective presentation.","What are the characteristics of an effective presentation?","Write a short speech for the inaugural session of the College IT Fair.","Write a short speech on the difficulty of being a student and employee.","Justify: success in oral reporting may determine professional success."],"skel":["One core message in one sentence","Three to five points, each with evidence","Pattern: chronological, causal, problem-solution","6x6 rule, 24 point type, charts not tables","Rehearse aloud and timed; back up the deck"],"answer":"Talk: 'Why our campus should adopt a single student portal'. Audience: 40 students and three administrators; eight minutes; purpose to persuade.\n\nCore message: one portal saves every student about four hours a semester.\n\nOpening, 45 seconds: hold up three different fee receipts and ask who has queued twice this month. State the purpose and the roadmap in one line.\n\nPoint 1, 90 seconds: the four systems students use now, shown as four login screens.\n\nPoint 2, two minutes: the four hours lost, from a survey of 120 students, on one bar chart.\n\nPoint 3, two minutes: what single sign-on cost two other campuses, and the one real risk, data migration.\n\nClose, 45 seconds: ask the administrators for a two-week pilot and name the start date.\n\nFive slides, six lines each, no paragraph text. Rehearsed twice aloud at 130 words a minute, with the deck on PDF and USB."},{"n":10,"name":"Recommendation Reports","hook":"The reader is not paying for analysis; they are paying for one defensible decision.","steps":[{"t":"Separate the problem from the request","d":"State what is actually wrong and who bears the cost. The reader must accept the problem before any option matters."},{"t":"Set criteria before looking at options","d":"Cost, time, risk, scalability, support. Criteria chosen afterwards always fit the option you already prefer."},{"t":"Evaluate every option against every criterion","d":"An options-by-criteria matrix with evidence in the cells; write 'unknown' where you lack data."},{"t":"Conclude, then recommend","d":"Conclusions say what the analysis shows; recommendations say what to do, in what order, by whom, by when."},{"t":"Name the trade-off and the risk","d":"One recommendation, honestly costed, with the mitigation you would use. Hedging reads as indecision."},{"t":"Package it for a decision-maker","d":"Transmittal memo, contents, executive summary, then the body; the summary may be all that is read."}],"keys":[{"k":"Recommendation report","v":"Analyses a problem, evaluates alternatives against criteria, recommends one course of action."},{"k":"Feasibility report","v":"A recommendation report answering only whether to proceed at all."},{"k":"Criteria","v":"The standards used to judge options, fixed before the options are examined."},{"k":"Options x criteria matrix","v":"Table with options as rows and criteria as columns, evidence in each cell."},{"k":"Executive summary","v":"One-page stand-alone digest: problem, method, findings, recommendation."},{"k":"Memo of transmittal","v":"Short covering note handing the report over and naming its purpose."},{"k":"Conclusions vs recommendations","v":"Conclusions state what the analysis shows; recommendations state what to do."},{"k":"Scope and limitations","v":"What the report covers, and which data or time constraints weaken it."},{"k":"Hedged recommendation","v":"A recommendation with no owner, date or cost; it fails the decision test."}],"traps":["Choosing criteria after the comparison so the favourite wins.","Recommending all three options 'depending on budget' and making no decision.","Writing findings as narrative instead of a matrix the reader can scan.","Skipping limitations, which makes every later criticism land on the writer."],"asks":["What is a recommendation report and how does it differ from a proposal?","Explain the structure of a recommendation report.","Write a recommendation report evaluating three cloud storage solutions for a 50-employee firm.","Write a recommendation report to Kathmandu Metropolitan City on improving waste management.","Distinguish a feasibility report from a recommendation report."],"skel":["Problem, criteria, findings, conclusions, recommendation","Criteria are fixed before options are examined","Options x criteria matrix with evidence in cells","One recommendation, costed, owned and dated","The executive summary may be the only page read"],"answer":"Recommendation: single sign-on student portal\n\nProblem. Students use four separate systems for admission, fees, results and library, re-entering credentials at each. A survey of 120 students found a median of 3.7 hours lost per semester to logins and password resets, and the helpdesk logs 62 reset tickets a month.\n\nCriteria. Annual cost under Rs 4 lakh, implementation within one semester, single sign-on with existing LDAP, mobile access, and a local support contract.\n\nAnalysis. Portal A meets all five criteria at Rs 3.6 lakh with a 14-week rollout. Portal B is cheaper at Rs 1.9 lakh but needs 26 weeks and has no local support. Portal C is the most capable but costs Rs 9.2 lakh, double the budget.\n\nRecommendation. Adopt Portal A from the next semester, with the IT unit owning migration and a pilot across two departments in weeks 1 to 4. Risk: directory data is unclean, so a two-week LDAP audit must precede rollout."},{"n":11,"name":"Proposals","hook":"A proposal is a price tag attached to a solution the reader has not yet agreed to want.","steps":[{"t":"Establish that a problem is worth money","d":"Quantify the cost of doing nothing before describing the solution. A proposal without a problem is a brochure."},{"t":"Decide solicited or unsolicited, internal or external","d":"That fixes format, length, and how much you must prove versus what the reader already accepts."},{"t":"Answer the reader's four questions","d":"What will you do, when, with whom, at what cost. Everything else is appendix material."},{"t":"Write the approach as work, not intention","d":"Deliverables, method, milestones and acceptance criteria. 'Improve quality' is not a statement of work."},{"t":"Price it openly","d":"Itemised budget with assumptions and a contingency line. Hidden cost discovered later kills the contract."},{"t":"Close by asking for a decision","d":"A named approval, a signature block and a validity date. A proposal that does not ask is a report."}],"keys":[{"k":"Proposal","v":"A persuasive request to solve a problem or do a piece of work, with cost and time."},{"k":"Solicited proposal","v":"Written in response to a published RFP; must follow the RFP's structure exactly."},{"k":"Unsolicited proposal","v":"Written without a request; must sell the problem before the solution."},{"k":"Internal proposal","v":"Memo or short letter inside the organisation, usually one to three pages."},{"k":"Statement of work","v":"The deliverables, method, milestones and acceptance criteria you commit to."},{"k":"Gantt chart","v":"Bar chart of tasks against calendar time, showing dependencies and milestones."},{"k":"Budget assumptions","v":"The rates, quantities and exclusions behind each line; they protect both parties."},{"k":"Executive summary","v":"One page: problem, solution, cost, benefit, ask; often the only page read."},{"k":"Letter of transmittal","v":"Cover letter that hands the proposal over and states its validity."},{"k":"Persuasion levers","v":"Credibility, reader benefit, evidence, realistic cost and risk mitigation."}],"traps":["Describing the solution for three pages before naming the problem.","Quoting one lump-sum price with no assumptions, then renegotiating.","Ignoring the RFP's required section order and being disqualified unread.","Promising a delivery date with no schedule behind it."],"asks":["Differentiate formal and informal proposals; give the structure of a formal proposal.","Write a short proposal comparing laptops from three companies, with time, cost and objectives.","Write a proposal convincing your parents to allow a vacation with friends.","Write a proposal for redesigning a company website.","What is a proposal? Explain solicited and unsolicited proposals."],"skel":["Proposal is a persuasive request with cost, time and deliverables","Solicited answers an RFP; unsolicited sells the problem first","Formal: transmittal, title, summary, introduction, work, schedule, staff, budget","Statement of work names deliverables and acceptance criteria","Close with a named approval and a validity date"],"answer":"PROPOSAL: Five laptops for the operations desk\n\nTO: Manager, Operations, National Bank of Nepal\nFROM: IT Support\nDATE: 13 November 2026\n\nProblem. The five shared desktops at the operations desk run Windows 7 and take eleven minutes to boot. Staff lose about 40 minutes each per day, and two machines failed last quarter, halting cheque clearing for a morning.\n\nProposal. Buy five 14-inch business laptops with an 8th-generation i5, 16 GB RAM, a 512 GB SSD, a three-year on-site warranty and docking stations, at an estimated Rs 6.4 lakh total.\n\nComparison. Dell Latitude 3440: Rs 1.28 lakh each, three-year on-site cover. HP ProBook 450: Rs 1.34 lakh, one-year warranty, better keyboard. Lenovo ThinkPad E14: Rs 1.22 lakh, two-year warranty, slower service in Kathmandu.\n\nRecommendation. Dell, for warranty coverage and next-day parts, delivered in three weeks.\n\nApproval requested by 20 November so the purchase falls in this quarter's budget."},{"n":12,"name":"Ethics in the Workplace","hook":"Ethics is the part of the job the rulebook left blank, and the document is where the blank gets filled.","steps":[{"t":"Notice that a rule does not decide it","d":"Ethics begins where the code, the law and the deadline disagree or say nothing at all."},{"t":"Get the facts before the story","d":"Establish what was known, when, by whom, and what the documentation actually says."},{"t":"Map the stakeholders","d":"Users, colleagues, employer, regulator, public. Each has an interest the decision will help or hurt."},{"t":"Test the options three ways","d":"Against the code, against the law, and against the consequences if the decision were published."},{"t":"Decide, act and write it down","d":"Name the decision, the reason and the person told. A memo creates the record that protects everyone."},{"t":"Review and change the system","d":"One case fixed is not a culture; training, protection and aligned incentives stop the repeat."}],"keys":[{"k":"Ethics","v":"Principles guiding right conduct where no rule fully decides the case."},{"k":"Ethical dilemma","v":"A choice between options that each violate some value; no cost-free answer exists."},{"k":"Conflict of interest","v":"A private interest that could bias professional judgement; disclose it or withdraw."},{"k":"Whistleblowing","v":"Reporting wrongdoing outside the chain of command when internal channels fail."},{"k":"Plagiarism","v":"Presenting another's words, data or ideas as your own, with or without intent."},{"k":"Culture of ethics","v":"Code, leadership modelling, training, protection, aligned incentives and consequences."},{"k":"Misleading graph","v":"A truncated axis or distorted area that exaggerates a difference; an ethics breach."},{"k":"Confidentiality","v":"Protecting client and personal data from unnecessary collection, use or disclosure."},{"k":"Diffusion of responsibility","v":"No single person feels accountable, so nobody reports; a structural cause of silence."},{"k":"Six-step dilemma process","v":"Recognise, gather facts, map stakeholders, evaluate, decide and act, review."}],"traps":["Answering 'be honest' as if the dilemma had no competing value.","Treating legality as the whole of ethics; much misconduct is perfectly legal.","Calling whistleblowing disloyalty without mentioning protection and exhausted channels.","Ignoring the writer's own duties: no fabricated data and no silent omission."],"asks":["What is ethics? How can an organisation create a culture of ethics?","Explain the importance of ethics in workplace communication.","What ethical dilemmas might technical writers face in the IT industry?","What steps should a writer take when confronted with inaccurate information or plagiarism?","Write a memo to your supervisor about inaccurate safety information in the documentation."],"skel":["Ethics decides what the rules leave open","Core values: honesty, accuracy, confidentiality, fairness, credit","Dilemma: recognise, facts, stakeholders, evaluate, act, review","Culture: code, example, training, protection, incentives","Writer's duty: no fabrication, no misleading graphs, disclose sources"],"answer":"MEMORANDUM\nTO: R. Thapa, Head of Engineering\nFROM: Technical Documentation Lead\nDATE: 13 November 2026\nSUBJECT: Inaccurate vulnerability guidance in v4.2 release notes\n\nThe release notes for v4.2 state that CVE-2026-3188 is mitigated by the default configuration. That is wrong. Testing on 9 November showed the default configuration remains exploitable; only the manual patch in KB-441 closes it.\n\nAbout 1,800 installations have downloaded the notes, and our support team has been quoting them. Users may therefore be leaving a known vulnerability open on the strength of our document.\n\nI recommend three actions today: publish a corrected note with an inline warning banner, email all v4.2 licence holders, and withdraw the printed quick-start guide from the warehouse. I have drafted the correction and can issue it within two hours of your approval.\n\nPlease confirm by 4 p.m. so the advisory goes out before the weekend."}],"ecom":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","hook":"A shop without walls still needs people, law, payments and partners — this unit names those parts and shows each one can fail.","steps":[{"t":"The transaction moves onto a network","d":"Buying and selling happens over TCP/IP instead of a counter, so the seller needs no shop and the buyer needs no travel."},{"t":"Distance stops being a cost","d":"Ubiquity, global reach and universal standards mean one page serves Kathmandu and Kansas at the same marginal cost."},{"t":"Information density shifts power to the buyer","d":"Prices, costs and reviews are visible to everyone at once, so margin survives only on service, selection or speed."},{"t":"New transaction pairs appear","d":"B2C, B2B, C2C, C2B, m-commerce and u-commerce are simply who sells to whom, on which device, with what mobility."},{"t":"Each pair needs five supporting layers","d":"People, public policy, marketing, support services and partnerships must all exist before a single order completes."},{"t":"Law arrives last, and the paper starts here","d":"ETA 2063 gives those electronic records and signatures legal force, which is why the unit opens with definition, framework and Act."}],"keys":[{"k":"E-commerce","v":"Buying and selling of goods, services and information over computer networks, chiefly the internet."},{"k":"E-business","v":"All digitally enabled transactions within and beyond the firm — internal process, CRM and supply chain included."},{"k":"Ubiquity","v":"The shop is available everywhere at any time, so the market escapes geography and opening hours."},{"k":"Information density","v":"Prices, costs and quality are visible to all buyers at once, pushing price toward cost."},{"k":"Richness","v":"Video, audio and text carry the marketing message with the depth of a face-to-face sales call."},{"k":"Pure e-commerce","v":"Product, process and delivery agent are all digital — an e-book bought and downloaded."},{"k":"Partial e-commerce","v":"At least one dimension is physical — ordered online, delivered by a van, as Daraz does."},{"k":"E-commerce framework","v":"Five layers: people, public policy, marketing and advertisement, support services, business partnerships."},{"k":"U-commerce","v":"Ubiquitous commerce: any device, any network, any location — the wider idea that contains m-commerce."},{"k":"Electronic Transactions Act 2063","v":"Nepal's 2006 Act giving legal force to electronic records, signatures and certifying authorities."}],"traps":["Calling e-business and e-commerce synonyms; e-business is the wider term that contains e-commerce.","Listing the eight features without an example; the paper asks what ubiquity or richness means, supported.","Treating u-commerce as m-commerce on a bigger screen — the difference is any device and any network.","Saying ETA 2063 legalises e-commerce generally; it legalises records and signatures and punishes computer offences.","Defining pure versus partial by who sells; it is defined by whether a dimension stays physical."],"asks":["How are people, public policy and support services shaping the e-commerce framework?","What do the properties like ubiquitous, richness and information density in e-commerce mean? Support with an example.","How pure e-commerce differs from partial e-commerce? Support your answer with proper examples.","Describe the factors that make U-commerce different from M-commerce.","Briefly explain the electronic transaction act of Nepal."],"skel":["Define e-commerce; contrast with e-business; one Nepali example","Draw five-layer framework: people, policy, marketing, support, partnerships","Eight features, one concrete example each","Pure vs partial; B2C, B2B, C2C, m-commerce, u-commerce","Nepal: Daraz, eSewa, cash on delivery, weak last mile","ETA 2063: e-records, digital signatures, offences, IT Tribunal"],"answer":"E-commerce is the buying and selling of goods, services and information over computer networks, chiefly the internet; e-business is wider, covering every digitally enabled transaction inside and beyond the firm — internal process, CRM, supply chain. The framework stacks five layers. At the centre sit people: buyers, sellers, intermediaries, IT staff and management. Public policy surrounds them — law, privacy, taxation and the Electronic Transactions Act 2063. Marketing and advertisement cover research, promotion and pricing. Support services supply the plumbing: payment systems, logistics, security, certification and hosting. Business partnerships — exchanges, affiliates, alliances — connect the firm to its supply chain. Each layer fails independently: strong marketing over a weak payment layer produces abandoned carts, which is why Nepali e-commerce still leans on cash on delivery. Pure e-commerce digitises all three dimensions, as with an e-book; partial keeps one physical, as with Daraz delivering a phone to Lalitpur."},{"n":2,"name":"E-commerce Business Model","hook":"Before a single product photo goes up, someone has to answer why anyone buys, how the money arrives, and who already does it better.","steps":[{"t":"Someone asks why anyone would buy","d":"The value proposition answers it, and every other element exists to make that answer true and repeatable."},{"t":"Then how the money actually arrives","d":"A revenue model names the mechanism — sale, subscription, transaction fee, advertising, affiliate, freemium — before costs are counted."},{"t":"The counterparty decides the shape","d":"Selling to consumers gives the seven B2C models; selling to firms gives net marketplaces and private industrial networks."},{"t":"B2B needs documents to move without people","d":"EDI sends purchase order, invoice and ASN between translators, so a large order reaches a supplier without being rekeyed."},{"t":"The firm sits inside a longer chain","d":"Industry value chain and firm value chain show where the margin in that document flow actually settles."},{"t":"So the paper asks elements, models and EDI","d":"Each question is one link of this argument, from value proposition down to the EDI translator and the VAN."}],"keys":[{"k":"Business model","v":"Set of planned activities designed to produce a profit in a marketplace."},{"k":"Value proposition","v":"The reason a customer buys from you rather than from the shop next door."},{"k":"Revenue model","v":"How activity is converted into money: sales, subscription, fee, advertising, affiliate, freemium."},{"k":"Competitive advantage","v":"A defensible edge — first mover, cost, scope, differentiation, data or network effects."},{"k":"E-tailer","v":"Sells goods directly online, pure-click or bricks-and-click, holding inventory and margin."},{"k":"Market creator","v":"Builds the marketplace and takes a fee from trades between buyers and sellers — Hamrobazar."},{"k":"Transaction broker","v":"Processes a transaction for a fee without owning the goods — Booking.com, eSewa."},{"k":"Net marketplace","v":"B2B hub where many buyers and sellers meet: e-distributor, e-procurement, exchange, consortium."},{"k":"EDI","v":"Computer-to-computer exchange of standard business documents between trading partners, replacing paper."},{"k":"VAN","v":"Value Added Network — the private carrier routing EDI messages between translators, now rivalled by AS2."}],"traps":["Reciting eight elements as a list; the marks sit in value proposition tied to revenue model.","Calling an exchange and an industry consortium the same; a consortium is owned by the industry it serves.","Saying EDI needs the internet; classic EDI ran on value added networks and still does.","Confusing revenue model with pricing model — one is where money comes from, the other how it is charged.","Giving only foreign examples for B2C; Daraz, SastoDeal and Hamrobazar cost nothing to add."],"asks":["What is e-commerce business model? Describe the key elements of e-commerce business model.","Explain different types of B2C business models with examples.","What is B2B e-commerce? Discuss various B2B business models.","What is EDI? Describe the significance of EDI in e-commerce? Justify with an example.","How are exchanges different from industry consortiums?","Explain the types of private industrial networks with examples."],"skel":["Business model vs business plan; the profit logic","Eight elements — value proposition first, management team last","Revenue models: sales, subscription, fee, ads, affiliate, freemium","B2C: e-tailer, community, content, portal, broker, market creator, service","B2B: e-distributor, e-procurement, exchange, consortium, private network","EDI layers: application, translator, VAN, partner translator"],"answer":"A business model is the set of planned activities designed to produce a profit in a marketplace; the business plan is the document that describes it. Eight elements carry it. The value proposition states why a customer buys from this firm — Daraz offers selection and delivery that a single shop cannot. The revenue model says how money arrives: sales, subscription, transaction fee, advertising, affiliate or freemium. Market opportunity sizes the addressable market and the money in it. The competitive environment names rivals, substitutes and barriers. Competitive advantage is the defensible edge — first mover, cost, scope, differentiation, data or network effects. Market strategy is the entry and promotion plan. Organisational development covers structure, team and partners. The management team supplies the experience that makes the rest credible. Weakness in any element surfaces as a weak business plan, and the examiner wants that linkage rather than eight definitions."},{"n":3,"name":"Electronic Payment System","hook":"Follow one click all the way to the merchant's bank account — every remaining topic in this unit exists because that path leaks.","steps":[{"t":"The click leaves the shopper","d":"Checkout turns the cart into an order total; the card details go to a payment gateway, never into the store's database."},{"t":"Authorisation puts a hold on funds","d":"Gateway to acquiring bank to card association to issuing bank, which checks the balance and answers approve, decline or referral."},{"t":"Capture turns the hold into a claim","d":"The merchant batches captured orders, usually at day end; capture is the step that creates the receivable."},{"t":"Settlement moves the actual money","d":"The issuer pays the acquirer through the association, and the acquirer pays the merchant net of the merchant discount rate."},{"t":"Every party on that path saw the card","d":"Too many hands see the number, which is the flaw SET was specified to remove."},{"t":"So SET splits order from payment","d":"A dual signature signs the two digests at once: merchant reads OI, bank reads PI, both verify one link — which is why the paper asks it."}],"keys":[{"k":"Payment gateway","v":"Merchant-facing service that encrypts card data and routes it to the acquirer, keeping the store out of PCI scope."},{"k":"Issuer vs acquirer","v":"Issuer holds the cardholder's account and pays; acquirer holds the merchant's account and receives."},{"k":"Authorisation","v":"Issuer's approve, decline or referral answer that reserves the amount without moving any money."},{"k":"Capture","v":"Merchant's request converting an authorisation into a real charge, usually batched at day end."},{"k":"Settlement","v":"Interbank transfer of funds, issuer to acquirer to merchant, net of the merchant discount rate."},{"k":"Dual signature","v":"Cardholder signs the hash of OI and PI together, so merchant reads OI, bank reads PI, both verify the link."},{"k":"Digital wallet","v":"App holding funds or linked cards: closed for one merchant, semi-closed without withdrawal, open with withdrawal."},{"k":"E-cash","v":"Token-based electronic money with blind signatures — anonymous and offline-capable, but open to double spending."},{"k":"Vickrey auction","v":"Sealed bids, highest wins but pays the second-highest, making truthful bidding dominant."},{"k":"EBPP","v":"Electronic bill presentment and payment, in three models: biller-direct, consolidator, bank-hosted."}],"traps":["Confusing authorisation with capture; the hold is not the charge, and settlement comes later still.","Drawing the card flow without the card association; Visa and Mastercard sit between the two banks.","Saying SET encrypts the card for the merchant; SET's point is that the merchant never receives it.","Describing a wallet as a bank account; a semi-closed wallet cannot be cashed out.","Calling Bitcoin e-cash; e-cash is issuer-backed and blind-signed, virtual currency has no central issuer."],"asks":["What is an e-payment system? How do online credit card transactions work?","What is the SET protocol? Explain its participants.","What is a dual signature? Describe the dual signature operations used during purchase request in SET.","What is a digital wallet? How does it work? Describe payment authorization and payment capture of SET.","Discuss how credit card transactions, electronic checks and stored value payment work, with pros and cons.","How auctioning is done in e-commerce? Discuss different types of auctioning."],"skel":["Define e-payment; draw cardholder, merchant, gateway, acquirer, association, issuer","Authorisation, capture, settlement — what moves at each","SET participants: cardholder, merchant, issuer, acquirer, gateway, CA","SET flow: both registrations, purchase request, dual signature, auth, capture","Dual signature: sign H(OI) and H(PI) together; each side sees one half","Nepal: eSewa, Khalti, connectIPS, Fonepay, cash on delivery still dominant"],"answer":"The chain begins when a shopper pays. Authorisation asks the issuing bank to hold the amount; capture converts that hold into a charge; settlement moves funds from issuer to acquirer to merchant, net of the merchant discount rate. Every hop sees the card number, which is why Visa and MasterCard specified SET. SET registers cardholder and merchant with a certificate authority, then sends a purchase request carrying order information and payment information as separate halves joined by a dual signature: the cardholder hashes OI and PI, concatenates both digests, signs the result, and sends that signature with each half. The merchant reads only the order, the acquirer reads only the card data, and both verify the same signature, so the link cannot be forged or repudiated. Payment authorisation and capture then run through the gateway. In Nepal the same job is done by eSewa, Khalti and connectIPS under NRB licensing, using TLS instead of SET."},{"n":4,"name":"Building E-commerce System","hook":"A store is three promises kept in order: the catalogue says what exists, the cart reserves it, the order proves the money moved.","steps":[{"t":"The catalogue becomes the source of truth","d":"Products, variants, prices and stock live in a database; a static catalogue hard-codes those facts into HTML and cannot keep up."},{"t":"The cart holds a claim, not a purchase","d":"Add-to-cart writes a session cart or a browser one; stock is not yet reserved, which is why carts get abandoned."},{"t":"Checkout freezes the claim into an order","d":"Tax and shipping are computed once, and the order enters a state machine: placed, paid, packed, shipped, delivered."},{"t":"Payment turns the order into paid","d":"The gateway answers by webhook; the store verifies the signature and uses an idempotency key so a retried callback cannot double-charge."},{"t":"The back office reconciles","d":"Stock is decremented, the invoice goes into ERP, and the day's gateway statement is matched against orders or the books drift."},{"t":"So the paper asks for the pieces","d":"Questions on static versus dynamic catalogue, carts, website elements and gateway integration ask which promise each component keeps."}],"keys":[{"k":"Static catalogue","v":"Hand-coded HTML product pages: cheap and simple, but no search, no personalisation, no scale."},{"k":"Dynamic catalogue","v":"Pages generated from a product database on request, so filtering, pricing and personalisation become possible."},{"k":"Server-side cart","v":"Cart stored in session or database; survives device change and login, at the cost of a write per action."},{"k":"Cart merging","v":"On login the anonymous cart is combined with the saved cart, so a shopper loses no basket."},{"k":"Order state machine","v":"Only legal transitions: cart, placed, paid, packed, shipped, delivered, returned — no skipping."},{"k":"Idempotency key","v":"A unique token per payment attempt, so a repeated callback is applied once and never twice."},{"k":"ACID","v":"Atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability — the four guarantees a payment write must hold."},{"k":"Webhook / IPN","v":"Server-to-server payment notification; must be signature-verified, never trusted from the returning browser."},{"k":"Headless commerce","v":"Storefront decoupled from the commerce back end, as in Saleor or Medusa, so each scales separately."},{"k":"PCI-DSS scope","v":"Never store the card number; hosted checkout or tokenisation keeps the merchant in the smallest audit scope."}],"traps":["Trusting the browser's return URL as proof of payment; only a verified server callback is evidence.","Saying the cart reserves stock; reservation happens at order placement or through an explicit stock lock.","Treating ERP integration as a nightly batch only, so stock and price drift through the working day.","Comparing WooCommerce with Shopify as equals: one is self-hosted software, the other is a subscription service.","Forgetting tax and shipping are computed at checkout, so the cart total and the charged total differ."],"asks":["Explain the development of e-commerce websites.","What are the basic elements to be considered while developing an e-commerce website?","What is a catalog in e-commerce application? How can you create catalogs?","Why is it important to build catalogs? How static catalog differs from dynamic catalog?","Discuss the post-purchase interaction involved in mercantile process from customers perspective."],"skel":["Components: storefront, back office, payments, logistics, CRM, analytics","Static vs dynamic catalogue; application server plus database","Cart: add, update, remove, coupon, tax, shipping, merge, abandon","Order state machine, ACID and idempotency","Gateway integration: hosted vs direct API, webhook, signature, refunds","CMS: WooCommerce, Magento, OpenCart, Shopify, headless"],"answer":"Building a store means building four layers. The storefront holds a dynamic catalogue generated from a product database, with search, filters and variants, so price and stock live in one place. The cart holds a claim rather than a purchase: add, update, remove, apply coupon, compute tax and shipping, and merge the anonymous cart on login. Checkout freezes the claim into an order that moves through a state machine — placed, paid, packed, shipped, delivered, returned — with ACID writes and an idempotency key so a retried payment callback cannot double-charge. The gateway is integrated through hosted checkout or a direct API, results arrive by signature-verified webhook, and refunds run the same path backwards. The back office decrements stock, pushes invoices into ERP and reconciles the gateway statement against orders. Around it sit analytics, logistics and CRM. Open-source options are WooCommerce, Magento, OpenCart and PrestaShop; Shopify is the hosted alternative."},{"n":5,"name":"Security in E-Commerce","hook":"Every hop the payment chain crosses is a door, and security is the discipline of knowing which door each mechanism locks.","steps":[{"t":"The chain has six doors","d":"Cardholder device, network, merchant server, gateway, bank and database each expose the same transaction to a different attacker."},{"t":"Threats map to doors","d":"Phishing and spyware work the cardholder, sniffing and pharming the network, injection and insider abuse the merchant, carding the stored data."},{"t":"Each door needs a named property","d":"Confidentiality for the wire, integrity for the amount, authenticity for both parties, non-repudiation for disputes, availability for the sale."},{"t":"Mechanisms are chosen by property","d":"TLS for confidentiality, a hash for integrity, a signature over that hash for non-repudiation, a certificate to bind key to name."},{"t":"Keys are the real weak point","d":"Hybrid encryption exists because RSA is slow: AES carries the bulk, the asymmetric key wraps the session key, trust rests on the certificate chain."},{"t":"Law makes it enforceable","d":"ETA 2063 gives electronic records and signatures legal standing and punishes unauthorised access, so a signature can settle a real dispute."}],"keys":[{"k":"Confidentiality","v":"Only authorised parties read the data, achieved by encryption in transit and at rest."},{"k":"Non-repudiation","v":"A party cannot later deny sending a message, achieved by a signature made with their private key."},{"k":"Digital signature","v":"Hash the message, encrypt the digest with the sender's private key; any change breaks verification."},{"k":"Digital certificate","v":"An X.509 structure binding a public key to an identity, signed by a trusted certificate authority."},{"k":"PKI","v":"Authorities, registration, revocation lists and policy that make public keys trustworthy at scale."},{"k":"Pharming","v":"Redirecting a domain at DNS or hosts level, so a correct address still lands on a fake site."},{"k":"Social engineering","v":"Manipulating a person rather than a system — pretexting, phishing, baiting, tailgating."},{"k":"IDS vs IPS","v":"IDS detects and alerts; IPS sits inline and blocks. Both may be host-based or network-based."},{"k":"Hybrid cryptography","v":"Asymmetric exchange of a symmetric session key, then fast AES for the payload itself."},{"k":"ETA 2063 offences","v":"Unauthorised access, damage, fraud, piracy and illegal content, with penalties and an IT Tribunal."}],"traps":["Saying a hash encrypts; it is one-way and cannot be reversed, so it proves integrity only.","Confusing authentication with non-repudiation — a password proves presence, a signature proves authorship.","Describing SSL as an encryption algorithm; it is a protocol using certificates and a negotiated cipher suite.","Answering a security question with a list of threats and no mechanism; each threat needs its countermeasure named.","Treating privacy as confidentiality; privacy is control over personal data, not secrecy of the transaction."],"asks":["What are the security threats in e-commerce? How client, server and data transaction securities are maintained?","How are cryptography and hash functions used in e-commerce to ensure security?","How are digital signatures used in e-commerce security?","What is non-repudiation? Which security mechanism can be used to ensure it? List the threats in e-commerce.","What is an intrusion? How intrusion detection systems are used to ensure security in e-commerce?","What is identity theft? How spoofing and pharming can impact e-commerce trades?"],"skel":["Six dimensions: confidentiality, integrity, availability, authenticity, non-repudiation, privacy","Five threats, each tied to a layer and an example","Client, server and transaction security measures","Symmetric vs asymmetric vs hybrid; hash properties","Signature plus certificate plus PKI chain of trust","ETA 2063 offences, penalties and IT Tribunal"],"answer":"Security is judged against six dimensions: confidentiality, integrity, availability, authenticity, non-repudiation and privacy. Threats attack them at different points. Malicious code, adware and spyware compromise the client; phishing and social engineering compromise the person; hacking, SQL injection, session hijacking and carding compromise the server and its data; sniffing and man-in-the-middle attack the wire; spoofing and pharming misdirect the visitor; insider abuse and chargeback fraud exploit process. Client security is patching, anti-malware, MFA, HTTPS-only browsing and no public Wi-Fi for payments. Server security is hardening, patching, a web application firewall, least privilege, logging, DMZ, IDS/IPS and penetration testing. Transaction security is TLS in transit, encryption and tokenisation at rest, salted hashing for passwords, digital signatures for non-repudiation, and never storing the card number. ETA 2063 supplies the legal teeth, making records and signatures admissible and unauthorised access an offence."},{"n":6,"name":"Digital Marketing","hook":"That first click was never free — someone bid for it in an auction that closed in under a hundred milliseconds.","steps":[{"t":"Traffic must be bought or earned","d":"Paid search, display, social, e-mail and affiliate each deliver a different kind of visitor at a different price per acquisition."},{"t":"Targeting decides who is worth bidding for","d":"Demographic, geographic, contextual, behavioural, lookalike and retargeting narrow the audience; cookies and device IDs make it possible."},{"t":"The impression itself is auctioned","d":"Ad exchange, DSP and SSP run real-time bidding per impression, and the winner pays CPM, CPC or CPA according to the contract."},{"t":"The click is measured","d":"Impressions, CTR, CPC, conversion rate and CAC turn that spend into a verdict for each channel and each creative."},{"t":"The verdict is compared with lifetime value","d":"A channel costing more than the customer's lifetime value is a leak, however healthy its click-through rate looks."},{"t":"So the paper asks models and metrics","d":"Pricing models, real-time bidding and the Facebook, Twitter, Pinterest and AdSense cases are one auction seen from four seats."}],"keys":[{"k":"CTR","v":"Clicks divided by impressions; measures how well the creative matches the audience."},{"k":"CPM","v":"Cost per thousand impressions — attention sold by the thousand, the publisher-side model."},{"k":"eCPM","v":"Revenue per thousand impressions whatever the pricing model, used to compare channels and placements."},{"k":"RTB","v":"Real-time bidding: each impression auctioned to advertisers in milliseconds as the page loads."},{"k":"DSP vs SSP","v":"Demand-side platform buys inventory for advertisers; supply-side platform sells it for publishers."},{"k":"Quality score","v":"Relevance, expected CTR and landing page rating, which together set ad rank and price per click."},{"k":"Affiliate marketing","v":"A publisher earns commission per sale or lead through a tracked link and a cookie window."},{"k":"Interstitial ad","v":"Full-screen ad between content pages; high impact, high irritation, worst on mobile."},{"k":"CAC vs LTV","v":"Acquisition cost per customer against the revenue that customer returns; the ratio decides viability."},{"k":"Programmatic advertising","v":"Automated buying and selling of ad inventory through exchanges, with or without real-time bidding."}],"traps":["Mixing CPM and CPC when computing spend; each formula has its own denominator.","Calling SEO a paid channel; search engine marketing contains SEO, but only the ads are bought.","Equating reach with impressions; reach counts people, impressions count views.","Describing real-time bidding as an ad format; it is a buying mechanism that can serve any format.","Quoting CTR as success without conversion — a cheap click that never buys is a loss."],"asks":["What is the cost per thousand (CPM)? How is eCPM used to measure return on investment from a digital ad?","What is display ad marketing? How do attrition, view-to-cart, acquisition and retention rates influence it?","What is digital marketing? Why is it essential? How do CTR, bounce-back rate and conversion rate influence e-mail marketing?","Discuss different pricing models for online advertisements.","How are social marketing, mobile marketing and local marketing used to promote business?","How is fan acquisition, amplification and brand strengthening done using Facebook marketing tools?"],"skel":["Define digital marketing; channels: search, display, social, e-mail, mobile, affiliate","Targeting types and the cookie and device-ID basis","Exchange to DSP and SSP, then real-time bidding in milliseconds","Pricing: CPM, CPC, CPA, CPL, CPV, flat, hybrid","Metrics: impressions, reach, CTR, conversion, CAC, ROAS","Cases: Meta Ads, X promoted posts, Pinterest pins, AdSense vs AdWords"],"answer":"Digital marketing is promotion through digital channels — search, display, social, e-mail, mobile, affiliate and content — and it matters because the same instrumentation that delivers an ad also prices it. Audience is chosen by targeting: demographic, geographic, contextual, behavioural, lookalike or retargeting, using cookies and device identifiers. Inventory is then sold through an exchange, where a demand-side platform bids for the advertiser and a supply-side platform sells for the publisher, often by real-time bidding per impression. Pricing follows the contract: CPM for attention, CPC for interest, CPA or CPL for action, CPV for video. Metrics judge the result — impressions and reach for exposure, CTR for the creative, conversion rate and CAC for efficiency, and ROAS against lifetime value for viability. In Nepal this is what a Daraz seller does with a boosted post and a small Google Ads budget."},{"n":7,"name":"Optimizing E-commerce Systems","hook":"Traffic that arrives and leaves is a cost; optimisation is the work of turning the same visitors into orders.","steps":[{"t":"The visitor must find you unpaid","d":"Crawler, index, rank: a page nothing links to and robots.txt blocks never enters the inverted index at all."},{"t":"Ranking treats links as votes","d":"PageRank reads an inbound link as endorsement, discounted by the linker's importance and divided by its outbound links."},{"t":"On-page work makes the page legible","d":"Title, H1, URL, meta description, alt text, internal links, structured data and Core Web Vitals each answer one ranking signal."},{"t":"Analytics shows where the funnel leaks","d":"Source, behaviour and funnel reports turn sessions into drop-off points: product page, cart, or the payment page."},{"t":"Recommendations widen the basket","d":"Collaborative filtering borrows other users' behaviour, content-based matches item features, and production systems combine both."},{"t":"So the paper pairs SEO with recommendation","d":"Both are one task — raise the odds a given visitor buys, once by being found and once by being shown the next item."}],"keys":[{"k":"Crawling","v":"Spiders follow links and fetch pages; robots.txt and the sitemap govern what they may reach."},{"k":"Indexing","v":"Parsed content stored in an inverted index mapping terms to pages, which is what ranking searches."},{"k":"PageRank","v":"PR(A) = (1-d) + d times the sum of PR(Ti)/C(Ti), with damping near 0.85 and iterative computation."},{"k":"On-page SEO","v":"Everything on the page: title, headings, URL, meta, content, alt text, internal links, speed, schema."},{"k":"Off-page SEO","v":"Signals from elsewhere: backlink quality and relevance, anchor text, mentions, guest posts, PR."},{"k":"Technical SEO","v":"Sitemap, robots.txt, canonicals, 301s, hreflang and crawl budget — the plumbing the rest depends on."},{"k":"Collaborative filtering","v":"Recommends from other users' behaviour on a user-item matrix; user-based or item-based, weak on cold start."},{"k":"Content-based filtering","v":"Recommends items whose features resemble what the user already liked; needs no other users."},{"k":"Cold start","v":"A new user or item has no history, so neither filtering method can say anything useful yet."},{"k":"UTM parameters","v":"Tags on a campaign URL telling Analytics which source, medium and campaign produced the visit."}],"traps":["Saying PageRank is the only ranking factor; relevance, freshness and user signals also shape the page.","Confusing crawling with indexing — a crawled page may still be excluded from the index.","Calling social shares a proven direct ranking factor; the evidence is weak, links are stronger.","Mixing precision and recall with CTR when judging recommendation quality.","Stuffing keywords everywhere; stuffing is penalised, placement and search intent matter more."],"asks":["What is the significance of page rank in search engine optimization? How can you increase the page rank of an e-commerce website?","Describe the working mechanism of search engines.","What is Search Engine Optimization? How on page SEO can be done?","Why is it essential to perform SEO? How off page SEO can be done?","What is the significance of Google Analytics and social media analytics in e-commerce? How are they done?","What is the significance of recommendation systems? How is collaborative filtering different from content based?"],"skel":["Crawl, index, rank, serve","On-page checklist; off-page link quality; technical plumbing","PageRank formula, damping 0.85, link farms penalised","Analytics: dimensions vs metrics, UTM, goals, funnels","Collaborative vs content-based vs hybrid; cold start and sparsity","Quality metrics: CTR, conversion lift, precision/recall, NDCG"],"answer":"Search engines work in three stages: crawlers fetch pages by following links, the parser stores terms in an inverted index, and ranking orders candidates by relevance and authority before results are served. On-page SEO shapes what is indexed — title, H1, URL, meta description, keyword placement, heading hierarchy, internal links, image alt text, structured data, Core Web Vitals and mobile rendering. Off-page SEO shapes authority — the quality and relevance of inbound links, anchor text, brand mentions and genuine PR, never link farms. Technical SEO keeps the plumbing sound: sitemap.xml, robots.txt, canonical tags, 301s, hreflang and crawl budget. PageRank formalises link authority, PR(A) = (1-d) + d times the sum of PR(Ti)/C(Ti), with damping near 0.85, so a page rises when important pages link to it and spread their weight. To raise it, publish content worth citing and earn relevant links."}]};
